8,102,63,10,55,48,58,45,32,87,104,97,116,32,119,97,115,32,116,104,101,32,104,97,114,100,101,115,116,32,112,97,114,116,32,97,98,111,117,116,32,117,115,105,110,103,32,116,104,101,32,101,110,103,105,110,101,63,10,55,49,58,45,32,73,102,32,121,111,117,32,104,97,100,32,116,111,32,114,101,109,111,118,101,32,111,114,32,111,118,101,114,104,97,117,108,32,111,110,101,32,116,104,105,110,103,32,102,114,111,109,32,101,110,103,105,110,101,44,32,119,104,97,116,32,119,111,117,100,108,32,116,104,97,116,32,98,101,63,10,55,50,58,45,32,73,102,32,121,111,117,32,99,111,117,108,100,32,99,104,97,110,103,101,32,111,110,101,32,116,104,105,110,103,32,97,98,111,117,116,32,116,104,101,32,101,110,103,105,110,101,44,32,119,104,97,116,32,119,111,117,108,100,32,105,116,32,98,101,63,10,55,52,58,80,114,111,109,112,116,32,70,101,101,100,98,97,99,107,32,10,55,54,58,45,32,87,104,97,116,32,100,105,100,32,121,111,117,32,108,105,107,101,32,109,111,115,116,32,97,98,111,117,116,32,116,104,105,115,32,112,114,111,109,112,116,63,10,55,55,58,45,32,87,104,97,116,32,119,97,115,32,99,111,110,102,117,115,105,110,103,63,32,10,55,56,58,45,32,87,104,97,116,32,119,97,115,32,116,104,101,32,104,97,114,100,101,115,116,32,116,104,105,110,103,32,116,111,32,100,111,63,32,10,55,57,58,45,32,73,102,32,116,104,101,114,101,32,119,97,115,32,111,110,101,32,116,101,115,116,32,121,111,117,32,99,111,117,108,100,32,111,102,32,114,117,110,44,32,119,104,97,116,32,119,111,117,108,100,32,116,104,97,116,32,116,101,115,116,32,98,101,63,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":52,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/TASK.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":1,\"content\":\"SOURCE: Mario Kart 64 for N64\"},{\"line_number\":3,\"content\":\"Game Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\"},{\"line_number\":5,\"content\":\"Package: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be include in the html file if that is easier.\"},{\"line_number\":7,\"content\":\"Tech Stack: Use Three.js R180, WEBGPU, no fallback, rapier 0.19.3 for physics, Web Audio, Gamepad API; The rest of the tech stack is yours to choose.\"},{\"line_number\":9,\"content\":\"Art Direction: Use a stylized cel-shaded look for the game.\"},{\"line_number\":11,\"content\":\"Animation: Animation technique and implementation is your choice\"},{\"line_number\":13,\"content\":\"Working Environment and deliverable:\"},{\"line_number\":14,\"content\":\"Create a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637] here you can keep all your dev files. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637].html in root when the goal has been completed. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory.\"},{\"line_number\":16,\"content\":\"Performance: Treat it as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\"},{\"line_number\":18,\"content\":\"Testing: Keep testing efficient and lightweight. No screenshots, visual review, judge/adversarial reviews, or subagents.\"},{\"line_number\":20,\"content\":\"## Goal\"},{\"line_number\":22,\"content\":\"Build an original spiritual successor to Mario Kart 64 for N64.\"},{\"line_number\":24,\"content\":\"Scope\"},{\"line_number\":26,\"content\":\"Create a feature-complete demo inspired by Mario Kart 64 for N64. Capture what makes the source distinctive while modernizing and improving its mechanics, pacing, and UX wherever it benefits the game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice / game engine. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\"},{\"line_number\":28,\"content\":\"Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \"},{\"line_number\":30,\"content\":\"Multiplayer: Include the option to be able to play the game demo up to 4-player multiplayer.\"},{\"line_number\":32,\"content\":\"Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the Mario Kart 64 for N64.\"},{\"line_number\":34,\"content\":\"## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\"},{\"line_number\":35,\"content\":\"Design all interfaces and interactions for a \\\"10-foot experience\\\" with a PS5-compatible controller as the primary input. Keyboard and mouse are secondary inputs that map to the controller's interaction model, not a separate desktop paradigm. Apply modern UX principles. Use your judgment to balance deep systemic information with clean, uncluttered presentation.\"},{\"line_number\":37,\"content\":\"Common Issue (~10%): Gamepad horizontal/vertical input is intermittently inverted. As a quick fix, add toggles in Settings to invert horizontal and vertical axes.\"},{\"line_number\":39,\"content\":\"Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\"},{\"line_number\":41,\"content\":\"Touch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button.\"},{\"line_number\":43,\"content\":\"## Done\"},{\"line_number\":44,\"content\":\"No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\"},{\"line_number\":46,\"content\":\"## Source Information\"},{\"line_number\":47,\"content\":\"CRTICAL: Put Source Information, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply, an in-game About/Credits screen, and an HTML comment at the top of the file. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\"},{\"line_number\":48,\"content\":\"- Date: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\"},{\"line_number\":49,\"content\":\"- Mario Kart 64 for N64\"},{\"line_number\":50,\"content\":\"- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\"},{\"line_number\":51,\"content\":\"- Art Direction: style used\"},{\"line_number\":52,\"content\":\"- Animation: system used\"},{\"line_number\":53,\"content\":\"- Testing: testing parameters allowed\"},{\"line_number\":54,\"content\":\"- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\"},{\"line_number\":55,\"content\":\"- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\"},{\"line_number\":56,\"content\":\"- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M  (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\"},{\"line_number\":58,\"content\":\"Testing Feedback\"},{\"line_number\":60,\"content\":\"- How many issues did testing catch?\"},{\"line_number\":61,\"content\":\"- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\"},{\"line_number\":62,\"content\":\"- What was the most helpful test you ran?\"},{\"line_number\":63,\"content\":\"- What was the least helpful test you ran? \"},{\"line_number\":65,\"content\":\"Engine Feedback\"},{\"line_number\":67,\"content\":\"- What did you like most about the engine?\"},{\"line_number\":68,\"content\":\"- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\"},{\"line_number\":69,\"content\":\"- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\"},{\"line_number\":70,\"content\":\"- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\"},{\"line_number\":71,\"content\":\"- If you had to remove or overhaul one thing from engine, what woudl that be?\"},{\"line_number\":72,\"content\":\"- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\"},{\"line_number\":74,\"content\":\"Prompt Feedback \"},{\"line_number\":76,\"content\":\"- What did you like most about this prompt?\"},{\"line_number\":77,\"content\":\"- What was confusing? \"},{\"line_number\":78,\"content\":\"- What was the hardest thing to do? \"},{\"line_number\":79,\"content\":\"- If there was one test you could of run, what would that test be?\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"76b48b21-c1a9-4b35-8aa7-92c2f0000599"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The task is a Mario Kart 64 spiritual successor.\n\nResearching Mario Kart 64 mechanics comes first. The plan must convert the objective into a structured implementation guide.\n\nTASK.md requires a single bundled HTML file in the folder root and a matching dev subfolder. The filename must use a randomly selected playful title drawn from fifty brainstormed options.\n\nThe technical stack is Three.js R180 with WebGPU and rapier 0.19.3 physics. Cel-shading, animation technique selection, and external CDN dependencies are permitted. Performance is unlimited with no FPS optimization. Testing must be lightweight and efficient, avoiding screenshots, visual review, adversarial reviews, or subagents. The demo should be a polished vertical slice that captures distinctive Mario Kart 64 features while modernizing mechanics, pacing, and UX.\n\nAttract mode loops an AI demo or scripted replay after ten seconds of idle input and exits on any input. Cheats are allowed.\n\nMultiplayer supports up to four players.\n\nThe UI targets a 10-foot controller-first design with PS5-compatible controllers as primary input. Keyboard and mouse map to the controller model. Settings toggle horizontal and vertical axis inversion. No dense web-style data screens appear. Touch-only devices display a controller or keyboard required message plus a settings button.\n\nThe final reply must contain Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback sections.\n\nThe plan must require an HTML comment at the top of the bundled file and an in-game About/Credits screen. The chat reply containing Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback is the implementer's responsibility, not a file verification criterion.\n\nThe plan must explicitly require the implementer's final reply to contain the Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback sections. Verifiers will check these in the chat output rather than inside the bundled HTML file.\n\nA conflict exists between the harness plan template and TASK.md. The template calls for a browser launch check with screenshots, but TASK.md explicitly forbids screenshots and visual review. The plan must resolve this tension while keeping testing efficient and lightweight.\n\nThe plan writer must preserve the OBJECTIVE's exact wording verbatim. Must-have requirements from TASK.md are treated as mandatory and cannot be discarded. If a requirement appears infeasible, it should still be kept and the conflict recorded under Risks.\n\nThe plan writer prompt is the assignment contract. The harness template's browser launch check can verify console errors and page load without screenshots. This satisfies the lightweight testing requirement while keeping the task constraints intact.\n\nThe plan will gate browser verification on a headless load that confirms zero page errors, a WebGPU canvas, and script execution. Pixel readback and screenshots are explicitly excluded from scope because TASK.md forbids visual review. Structural tests, unit tests, and a clean console load will serve as the verification bar. The prohibition against judge or adversarial reviews applies to the implementer's testing process rather than the harness itself. Research into Mario Kart 64 defining mechanics has begun.\n\nThe research now identifies the core mechanics to port. The game features multiple racers including AI opponents. Item boxes grant power-ups such as shells, bananas, mushrooms, stars, lightning, and boo. The signature drift mechanic is the mini-turbo or power slide. Laps are completed in typical three-lap races with a clear finish order. Coins were removed from this title; it relies on item boxes instead.\n\nBattle mode was a distinctive feature of Mario Kart 64. The plan must decide whether it is essential to the spirit of the source. A kart racer with item boxes, laps, positions, and drifting boosts is recognizably Mario Kart without it.","signature":""},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-2","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Mario Kart 64 defining gameplay mechanics items drifting mini-turbo race structure modes"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-2","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mini-Turbo","title":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mini-Turbo"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://superluigibros.com/mario-kart-64/","title":"https://superluigibros.com/mario-kart-64/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.mariomayhem.com/consoles/64/mario_kart_64.php","title":"https://www.mariomayhem.com/consoles/64/mario_kart_64.php"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mario_Kart_64","title":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mario_Kart_64"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mario_Raceway","title":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Mario_Raceway"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://manuals.plus/m/c2cae59038e1218573487a4eff2ea117d0ab541a709280ddc4343df9c4fbc644","title":"https://manuals.plus/m/c2cae59038e1218573487a4eff2ea117d0ab541a709280ddc4343df9c4fbc644"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://en.wikipedia-on-ipfs.org/wiki/Mario_Kart_64","title":"https://en.wikipedia-on-ipfs.org/wiki/Mario_Kart_64"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Royal_Raceway","title":"https://www.mariowiki.com/Royal_Raceway"}]},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-3","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Mario Kart 64 N64 controls power slide item list cups laps battle mode distinctive features"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-3","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/n64/197860-mario-kart-64/faqs/3253","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/n64/197860-mario-kart-64/faqs/3253"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Mario_Kart_64/Controls","title":"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Mario_Kart_64/Controls"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/n64/197860-mario-kart-64/faqs/3254","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/n64/197860-mario-kart-64/faqs/3254"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://superluigibros.com/mario-kart-64/","title":"https://superluigibros.com/mario-kart-64/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://manuals.plus/m/c2cae59038e1218573487a4eff2ea117d0ab541a709280ddc4343df9c4fbc644","title":"https://manuals.plus/m/c2cae59038e1218573487a4eff2ea117d0ab541a709280ddc4343df9c4fbc644"}]},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-4","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Three.js r180 WebGPU import CDN @three.js version 180"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_5c0a8dc3-0f55-98ed-8b97-882c485dd6bc_call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-4","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebGPU.html","title":"https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebGPU.html"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/releases/tag/r180","title":"https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/releases/tag/r180"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/src/","title":"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/src/"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Mario Kart 64’s defining race, item, and mode mechanics are clear enough to fold into a short outcome contract. I’ll write the plan next."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-5","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/goal/plan-01a17fba-319e-7432-a807-eb7eb128c144.md","content":"# Plan: Single-file cel-shaded WebGPU spiritual successor to Mario Kart 64\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. The work root contains one bundled `.html` deliverable named `{Slug}-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html`, and a dev subfolder with that same basename holds the sources. `{Slug}` is the hyphenated form of an original playful riff on \"Mario Kart 64\" (not the trademark as the product title), chosen by running a real RNG over at least 50 distinct candidates; the comment at the top of the HTML lists all 50 and the RNG result. The page may load CDN dependencies. It has no TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. That same top comment and an in-game About/Credits screen both include Source Information (date `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM`, source \"Mario Kart 64 for N64\", tech with versions, art direction, animation system, testing parameters, environment, model, usage), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, with \"unknown\" where unconfirmed. The implementer's final reply includes Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback.\n2. The page uses Three.js r180 (npm `three@0.180`) with WebGPU only: if WebGPU or a secure context is missing it shows that WebGPU and a local server are required and does not start a WebGL renderer. Rapier 0.19.3 steps the world the race uses. Web Audio plays sounds for countdown, driving, item use, and impacts. Scene materials are cel-shaded (quantized/toon lighting, not default smooth PBR only). The render loop animates karts, drivers, or items over time. UI copy is original and lightly playful, in the spirit of a kart-racing manual, not copied Nintendo text. The on-screen product title is the chosen riff.\n3. Shipped race logic, driven from a fresh start state, supports accelerate, brake, reverse, steer, and hop; a drift that charges through at least two stages and releases into a short mini-turbo; a countdown rocket start in a valid window and a worse start on an early press; slower off-road than on-track; kart–kart and kart–wall collision response; a closed course raced for 3 laps by a field of 8; at least three selectable original characters in different weight/stat classes; CPU racers that follow the course and can use items; live rank; a finish order when the race ends. In-race state exposed to the HUD includes lap, position, speed, and the held item.\n4. Item boxes can be collected and then respawn. Collection runs a roulette the player can stop early, drawn from a pool weighted by position so leaders tend to get weaker items. A held item can be used and, for the shield-style classes, trailed behind. Shipped logic applies a distinct, observable effect for each class (original names allowed): straight ricochet projectile, homing projectile, leader-seeking projectile, dropped trap, trap bunch, decoy box, single speed boost, multi-charge boost, timed repeated boost, timed invincibility that knocks others aside, field-wide shrink or slow that spares the user, and a steal or disruptor aimed at a rival.\n5. From a controller-focused title menu the player can start: a cup of at least two original 3-lap courses against CPU, with placement points and a must-place-well-enough continue rule; a versus race for 2, 3, and 4 local players; a time trial with no rival item boxes, a starting multi-boost, and an in-session best time; and a balloon battle for 2–4 players on an arena (three balloons each, out at zero, last remaining wins). Play shows 2-, 3-, and 4-way split views. Gamepad is the primary input (PS5-compatible controller). Keyboard and mouse, if used, fire the same actions (steer, accelerate, brake, hop/drift, use item, camera, pause, menu move/confirm/back), not a separate desktop UI. Settings toggles invert horizontal and vertical gamepad axes and actually flip those axes. A touch-only device gets no touch driving controls and is shown \"Controller or keyboard required\" plus a settings control. After 10 seconds with no input on the title/menu, a looping attract demo (AI or scripted replay; it may cheat) starts and shows gameplay; any input exits it. Menus are sparse 10-foot screens, not dense web-style data pages.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: Inspect the root HTML and dev subfolder. Pass only if the filename and folder match criterion 1, the top comment lists 50 titles plus the RNG pick and the Source Information / Testing Feedback / Engine Feedback / Prompt Feedback blocks, the About/Credits markup contains those same blocks, and a search of the shipped page and bundled sources shows no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or stubbed feature. Also confirm `three@0.180` (or an equivalent r180 pin), a WebGPU renderer, no `WebGLRenderer` fallback path, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, `getGamepads` / Gamepad API, a cel/toon material or ramp, an animation update in the render loop, the exact string \"Controller or keyboard required\", invert-axis settings, and a 10-second attract idle threshold.\n2. gating: Add or update lightweight in-repo tests that import the same shipped modules the page bundles (not a copy and not a reimplementation) and start each case from a fresh state. Assert: rocket-start success versus early penalty; drift charge stages then a mini-turbo speed change on release; off-road slower than on-track; lap increment and rank across an 8-kart field; each item class changes victim or user state (speed, control, balloons, or held item) as in criterion 4; roulette position bias favors weaker items for the leader; cup continue versus retry; battle elimination at zero balloons; attract begins at 10s idle and clears on input; invert-horizontal and invert-vertical each negate only that axis. One test loads Rapier 0.19.3 and shows a dynamic body moves after steps taken through the shipped physics setup. Run the suite twice. Both runs pass with the same assertions. Write stdout/stderr to `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-2.log`.\n3. gating: Serve the real root HTML over localhost HTTP (WebGPU needs a secure context; `file://` must not be the launch path). If a headless browser is available (Playwright is expected), load the page twice and capture console plus DOM text to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Both runs must agree. Pass only if the visible product title is the chosen riff (not \"Mario Kart\", \"TODO\", or an empty title) and a menu control to start play is present, with no unexpected page error; the canvas drawing buffer matches its layout size when WebGPU initializes. If WebGPU or a secure context is unavailable, the page must show that WebGPU and a local server are required rather than a blank screen or a WebGL view. Do not take screenshots and do not grade pixels (TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review). If the browser cannot start for environmental reasons, save that failure output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; criteria 1–2 static checks plus the unit tests are then the accepted bar. A load that succeeds but is blank with no explanatory message is a product failure, not an environment fallback.\n4. evidence: Note in `{SCRATCH}/stack-notes.md` the cel-shade technique, the animation technique, and which menu entries open cup, versus, time trial, and battle. Absence of this note alone does not fail the goal if steps 1–3 passed.\n\n## Non-goals\n- The full MK64 roster (16 courses, four cups, 50/100/150cc, mirror/extra mode), slipstreaming, snaking balance, and saved ghost replays.\n- Online multiplayer, touch driving controls, desktop cursor menus, FPS targets, and rendering optimization.\n- Nintendo-owned names, courses, music, manual text, or assets (the source name belongs in the filename and credits only).\n- Screenshots, pixel/visual review, adversarial review passes, and subagents. TASK.md forbids them for this build.\n- Inlining CDN libraries into the HTML when a pinned external URL is enough.\n\n## Assumed scope\n- Work root is this folder (currently only `TASK.md`). New dev subfolder plus one root HTML file as named in criterion 1.\n- Template token SOURCE (\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\") is hyphenated to `Mario-Kart-64-for-N64` so the deliverable is one path segment. `{Slug}` is letters, digits, and hyphens only.\n- CDN pins: `three@0.180` WebGPU entry and `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` (or the official Rapier 0.19.3 browser build). No other physics library and no WebGL fallback.\n- Pure race, item, mode, attract, and input-mapping logic lives in modules the page bundle and the node tests both load. Course geometry, materials, audio buffers, and the HTML shell may stay in the dev folder until bundled.\n- Original content scale for the slice: at least two race courses, one battle arena, and at least three characters. Not the retail course list.\n\n## Implementation approach\nKeep rules (input mapping including invert, kart handling, laps and rank, item roulette and effects, cup/versus/time-trial/battle transitions, attract timer) in pure functions with no DOM, WebGPU, or audio inside them so node tests call the real code. A thin shell owns the Rapier world, Three.js r180 WebGPU scene, cel materials, animation update, Web Audio triggers, gamepad polling, and split-view cameras, and calls those functions each frame. Bundle the shell and modules into the single root HTML; load Three and Rapier from pinned CDNs if easier. On `file://` or a missing WebGPU adapter, show the server/WebGPU requirement and stop. Pick the title by executing an RNG over the 50 riffs and write the list plus the pick into the HTML comment. Fill About/Credits from the same text.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Run an RNG over 50 original riff titles, record the pick, and create the dev subfolder plus the named root HTML shell.\n- [ ] Implement pure input, race, item, mode, and attract logic, then the node tests in verification step 2.\n- [ ] Add two courses, one battle arena, original characters, Rapier 0.19.3 kart collision, cel-shaded WebGPU scene, animation update, HUD, and Web Audio cues.\n- [ ] Wire the 10-foot menu, invert-axis settings, 2/3/4-player splits, cup, versus, time trial, balloon battle, pause, attract mode, touch-only message, and About/Credits.\n- [ ] Bundle to the single root HTML with r180 WebGPU and Rapier 0.19.3, no WebGL fallback, and a clear file:// / no-WebGPU message. Remove every TODO, placeholder, and stub.\n- [ ] Run the unit suite twice and the localhost launch twice; write the logs under `{SCRATCH}`. Put Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment, the About screen, and the final reply.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- TASK.md requires WebGPU with no fallback, and WebGPU needs a secure context plus an adapter. Headless Chromium here may lack a working adapter. Do not satisfy that by adding WebGL. Use the launch escape hatch in verification step 3 when the browser cannot start or cannot create an adapter; a blank successful load is still a failure.\n- TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review, which conflicts with a pixel-fill launch gate. The launch gate is DOM text, console errors, and canvas buffer size only.\n- The filename template in TASK.md contains spaces (`GameTitle-SOURCE-...`, SOURCE = \"Mario Kart 64 for N64\"). This plan hyphenates those spaces so the path is one segment. A file that keeps the spaces but is otherwise identical still meets the template; either form is acceptable if the fixed suffix `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html` and the source token are present.\n- The final chat reply is not in the repo. Verifiers confirm Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment and About/Credits screen; the reply requirement is the implementer's and cannot be proven from files alone.\n- Retail MK64 mode count and course count would make the slice unfinishable. Criterion 5 is the defining subset (cup of two courses, versus, time trial, one balloon arena, 8-kart field, full item classes). Anything beyond that is a non-goal, not a silent drop.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11411,"output_tokens":17669,"cache_read_input_tokens":33280,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"d63753ad-fa62-4fec-a9c5-2b809070f1cc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-5a1830ca-653c-4482-93c0-d7a4fdf4489c-5","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Single-file cel-shaded WebGPU spiritual successor to Mario Kart 64\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work root contains one bundled `.html` deliverable named `{Slug}-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html`, and a dev subfolder with that same basename holds the sources. `{Slug}` is the hyphenated form of an original playful riff on \\\"Mario Kart 64\\\" (not the trademark as the product title), chosen by running a real RNG over at least 50 distinct candidates; the comment at the top of the HTML lists all 50 and the RNG result. The page may load CDN dependencies. It has no TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. That same top comment and an in-game About/Credits screen both include Source Information (date `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM`, source \\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\", tech with versions, art direction, animation system, testing parameters, environment, model, usage), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, with \\\"unknown\\\" where unconfirmed. The implementer's final reply includes Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback.\\n2. The page uses Three.js r180 (npm `three@0.180`) with WebGPU only: if WebGPU or a secure context is missing it shows that WebGPU and a local server are required and does not start a WebGL renderer. Rapier 0.19.3 steps the world the race uses. Web Audio plays sounds for countdown, driving, item use, and impacts. Scene materials are cel-shaded (quantized/toon lighting, not default smooth PBR only). The render loop animates karts, drivers, or items over time. UI copy is original and lightly playful, in the spirit of a kart-racing manual, not copied Nintendo text. The on-screen product title is the chosen riff.\\n3. Shipped race logic, driven from a fresh start state, supports accelerate, brake, reverse, steer, and hop; a drift that charges through at least two stages and releases into a short mini-turbo; a countdown rocket start in a valid window and a worse start on an early press; slower off-road than on-track; kart–kart and kart–wall collision response; a closed course raced for 3 laps by a field of 8; at least three selectable original characters in different weight/stat classes; CPU racers that follow the course and can use items; live rank; a finish order when the race ends. In-race state exposed to the HUD includes lap, position, speed, and the held item.\\n4. Item boxes can be collected and then respawn. Collection runs a roulette the player can stop early, drawn from a pool weighted by position so leaders tend to get weaker items. A held item can be used and, for the shield-style classes, trailed behind. Shipped logic applies a distinct, observable effect for each class (original names allowed): straight ricochet projectile, homing projectile, leader-seeking projectile, dropped trap, trap bunch, decoy box, single speed boost, multi-charge boost, timed repeated boost, timed invincibility that knocks others aside, field-wide shrink or slow that spares the user, and a steal or disruptor aimed at a rival.\\n5. From a controller-focused title menu the player can start: a cup of at least two original 3-lap courses against CPU, with placement points and a must-place-well-enough continue rule; a versus race for 2, 3, and 4 local players; a time trial with no rival item boxes, a starting multi-boost, and an in-session best time; and a balloon battle for 2–4 players on an arena (three balloons each, out at zero, last remaining wins). Play shows 2-, 3-, and 4-way split views. Gamepad is the primary input (PS5-compatible controller). Keyboard and mouse, if used, fire the same actions (steer, accelerate, brake, hop/drift, use item, camera, pause, menu move/confirm/back), not a separate desktop UI. Settings toggles invert horizontal and vertical gamepad axes and actually flip those axes. A touch-only device gets no touch driving controls and is shown \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" plus a settings control. After 10 seconds with no input on the title/menu, a looping attract demo (AI or scripted replay; it may cheat) starts and shows gameplay; any input exits it. Menus are sparse 10-foot screens, not dense web-style data pages.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Inspect the root HTML and dev subfolder. Pass only if the filename and folder match criterion 1, the top comment lists 50 titles plus the RNG pick and the Source Information / Testing Feedback / Engine Feedback / Prompt Feedback blocks, the About/Credits markup contains those same blocks, and a search of the shipped page and bundled sources shows no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or stubbed feature. Also confirm `three@0.180` (or an equivalent r180 pin), a WebGPU renderer, no `WebGLRenderer` fallback path, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, `getGamepads` / Gamepad API, a cel/toon material or ramp, an animation update in the render loop, the exact string \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", invert-axis settings, and a 10-second attract idle threshold.\\n2. gating: Add or update lightweight in-repo tests that import the same shipped modules the page bundles (not a copy and not a reimplementation) and start each case from a fresh state. Assert: rocket-start success versus early penalty; drift charge stages then a mini-turbo speed change on release; off-road slower than on-track; lap increment and rank across an 8-kart field; each item class changes victim or user state (speed, control, balloons, or held item) as in criterion 4; roulette position bias favors weaker items for the leader; cup continue versus retry; battle elimination at zero balloons; attract begins at 10s idle and clears on input; invert-horizontal and invert-vertical each negate only that axis. One test loads Rapier 0.19.3 and shows a dynamic body moves after steps taken through the shipped physics setup. Run the suite twice. Both runs pass with the same assertions. Write stdout/stderr to `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-2.log`.\\n3. gating: Serve the real root HTML over localhost HTTP (WebGPU needs a secure context; `file://` must not be the launch path). If a headless browser is available (Playwright is expected), load the page twice and capture console plus DOM text to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Both runs must agree. Pass only if the visible product title is the chosen riff (not \\\"Mario Kart\\\", \\\"TODO\\\", or an empty title) and a menu control to start play is present, with no unexpected page error; the canvas drawing buffer matches its layout size when WebGPU initializes. If WebGPU or a secure context is unavailable, the page must show that WebGPU and a local server are required rather than a blank screen or a WebGL view. Do not take screenshots and do not grade pixels (TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review). If the browser cannot start for environmental reasons, save that failure output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; criteria 1–2 static checks plus the unit tests are then the accepted bar. A load that succeeds but is blank with no explanatory message is a product failure, not an environment fallback.\\n4. evidence: Note in `{SCRATCH}/stack-notes.md` the cel-shade technique, the animation technique, and which menu entries open cup, versus, time trial, and battle. Absence of this note alone does not fail the goal if steps 1–3 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- The full MK64 roster (16 courses, four cups, 50/100/150cc, mirror/extra mode), slipstreaming, snaking balance, and saved ghost replays.\\n- Online multiplayer, touch driving controls, desktop cursor menus, FPS targets, and rendering optimization.\\n- Nintendo-owned names, courses, music, manual text, or assets (the source name belongs in the filename and credits only).\\n- Screenshots, pixel/visual review, adversarial review passes, and subagents. TASK.md forbids them for this build.\\n- Inlining CDN libraries into the HTML when a pinned external URL is enough.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root is this folder (currently only `TASK.md`). New dev subfolder plus one root HTML file as named in criterion 1.\\n- Template token SOURCE (\\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\") is hyphenated to `Mario-Kart-64-for-N64` so the deliverable is one path segment. `{Slug}` is letters, digits, and hyphens only.\\n- CDN pins: `three@0.180` WebGPU entry and `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` (or the official Rapier 0.19.3 browser build). No other physics library and no WebGL fallback.\\n- Pure race, item, mode, attract, and input-mapping logic lives in modules the page bundle and the node tests both load. Course geometry, materials, audio buffers, and the HTML shell may stay in the dev folder until bundled.\\n- Original content scale for the slice: at least two race courses, one battle arena, and at least three characters. Not the retail course list.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep rules (input mapping including invert, kart handling, laps and rank, item roulette and effects, cup/versus/time-trial/battle transitions, attract timer) in pure functions with no DOM, WebGPU, or audio inside them so node tests call the real code. A thin shell owns the Rapier world, Three.js r180 WebGPU scene, cel materials, animation update, Web Audio triggers, gamepad polling, and split-view cameras, and calls those functions each frame. Bundle the shell and modules into the single root HTML; load Three and Rapier from pinned CDNs if easier. On `file://` or a missing WebGPU adapter, show the server/WebGPU requirement and stop. Pick the title by executing an RNG over the 50 riffs and write the list plus the pick into the HTML comment. Fill About/Credits from the same text.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Run an RNG over 50 original riff titles, record the pick, and create the dev subfolder plus the named root HTML shell.\\n- [ ] Implement pure input, race, item, mode, and attract logic, then the node tests in verification step 2.\\n- [ ] Add two courses, one battle arena, original characters, Rapier 0.19.3 kart collision, cel-shaded WebGPU scene, animation update, HUD, and Web Audio cues.\\n- [ ] Wire the 10-foot menu, invert-axis settings, 2/3/4-player splits, cup, versus, time trial, balloon battle, pause, attract mode, touch-only message, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Bundle to the single root HTML with r180 WebGPU and Rapier 0.19.3, no WebGL fallback, and a clear file:// / no-WebGPU message. Remove every TODO, placeholder, and stub.\\n- [ ] Run the unit suite twice and the localhost launch twice; write the logs under `{SCRATCH}`. Put Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment, the About screen, and the final reply.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md requires WebGPU with no fallback, and WebGPU needs a secure context plus an adapter. Headless Chromium here may lack a working adapter. Do not satisfy that by adding WebGL. Use the launch escape hatch in verification step 3 when the browser cannot start or cannot create an adapter; a blank successful load is still a failure.\\n- TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review, which conflicts with a pixel-fill launch gate. The launch gate is DOM text, console errors, and canvas buffer size only.\\n- The filename template in TASK.md contains spaces (`GameTitle-SOURCE-...`, SOURCE = \\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\"). This plan hyphenates those spaces so the path is one segment. A file that keeps the spaces but is otherwise identical still meets the template; either form is acceptable if the fixed suffix `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html` and the source token are present.\\n- The final chat reply is not in the repo. Verifiers confirm Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment and About/Credits screen; the reply requirement is the implementer's and cannot be proven from files alone.\\n- Retail MK64 mode count and course count would make the slice unfinishable. Criterion 5 is the defining subset (cup of two courses, versus, time trial, one balloon arena, 8-kart field, full item classes). Anything beyond that is a non-goal, not a silent drop.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/goal/plan-01a17fba-319e-7432-a807-eb7eb128c144.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/goal/plan-01a17fba-319e-7432-a807-eb7eb128c144.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/goal/plan-01a17fba-319e-7432-a807-eb7eb128c144.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Single-file cel-shaded WebGPU spiritual successor to Mario Kart 64\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work root contains one bundled `.html` deliverable named `{Slug}-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html`, and a dev subfolder with that same basename holds the sources. `{Slug}` is the hyphenated form of an original playful riff on \\\"Mario Kart 64\\\" (not the trademark as the product title), chosen by running a real RNG over at least 50 distinct candidates; the comment at the top of the HTML lists all 50 and the RNG result. The page may load CDN dependencies. It has no TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. That same top comment and an in-game About/Credits screen both include Source Information (date `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM`, source \\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\", tech with versions, art direction, animation system, testing parameters, environment, model, usage), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback, with \\\"unknown\\\" where unconfirmed. The implementer's final reply includes Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback.\\n2. The page uses Three.js r180 (npm `three@0.180`) with WebGPU only: if WebGPU or a secure context is missing it shows that WebGPU and a local server are required and does not start a WebGL renderer. Rapier 0.19.3 steps the world the race uses. Web Audio plays sounds for countdown, driving, item use, and impacts. Scene materials are cel-shaded (quantized/toon lighting, not default smooth PBR only). The render loop animates karts, drivers, or items over time. UI copy is original and lightly playful, in the spirit of a kart-racing manual, not copied Nintendo text. The on-screen product title is the chosen riff.\\n3. Shipped race logic, driven from a fresh start state, supports accelerate, brake, reverse, steer, and hop; a drift that charges through at least two stages and releases into a short mini-turbo; a countdown rocket start in a valid window and a worse start on an early press; slower off-road than on-track; kart–kart and kart–wall collision response; a closed course raced for 3 laps by a field of 8; at least three selectable original characters in different weight/stat classes; CPU racers that follow the course and can use items; live rank; a finish order when the race ends. In-race state exposed to the HUD includes lap, position, speed, and the held item.\\n4. Item boxes can be collected and then respawn. Collection runs a roulette the player can stop early, drawn from a pool weighted by position so leaders tend to get weaker items. A held item can be used and, for the shield-style classes, trailed behind. Shipped logic applies a distinct, observable effect for each class (original names allowed): straight ricochet projectile, homing projectile, leader-seeking projectile, dropped trap, trap bunch, decoy box, single speed boost, multi-charge boost, timed repeated boost, timed invincibility that knocks others aside, field-wide shrink or slow that spares the user, and a steal or disruptor aimed at a rival.\\n5. From a controller-focused title menu the player can start: a cup of at least two original 3-lap courses against CPU, with placement points and a must-place-well-enough continue rule; a versus race for 2, 3, and 4 local players; a time trial with no rival item boxes, a starting multi-boost, and an in-session best time; and a balloon battle for 2–4 players on an arena (three balloons each, out at zero, last remaining wins). Play shows 2-, 3-, and 4-way split views. Gamepad is the primary input (PS5-compatible controller). Keyboard and mouse, if used, fire the same actions (steer, accelerate, brake, hop/drift, use item, camera, pause, menu move/confirm/back), not a separate desktop UI. Settings toggles invert horizontal and vertical gamepad axes and actually flip those axes. A touch-only device gets no touch driving controls and is shown \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" plus a settings control. After 10 seconds with no input on the title/menu, a looping attract demo (AI or scripted replay; it may cheat) starts and shows gameplay; any input exits it. Menus are sparse 10-foot screens, not dense web-style data pages.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Inspect the root HTML and dev subfolder. Pass only if the filename and folder match criterion 1, the top comment lists 50 titles plus the RNG pick and the Source Information / Testing Feedback / Engine Feedback / Prompt Feedback blocks, the About/Credits markup contains those same blocks, and a search of the shipped page and bundled sources shows no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or stubbed feature. Also confirm `three@0.180` (or an equivalent r180 pin), a WebGPU renderer, no `WebGLRenderer` fallback path, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, `getGamepads` / Gamepad API, a cel/toon material or ramp, an animation update in the render loop, the exact string \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", invert-axis settings, and a 10-second attract idle threshold.\\n2. gating: Add or update lightweight in-repo tests that import the same shipped modules the page bundles (not a copy and not a reimplementation) and start each case from a fresh state. Assert: rocket-start success versus early penalty; drift charge stages then a mini-turbo speed change on release; off-road slower than on-track; lap increment and rank across an 8-kart field; each item class changes victim or user state (speed, control, balloons, or held item) as in criterion 4; roulette position bias favors weaker items for the leader; cup continue versus retry; battle elimination at zero balloons; attract begins at 10s idle and clears on input; invert-horizontal and invert-vertical each negate only that axis. One test loads Rapier 0.19.3 and shows a dynamic body moves after steps taken through the shipped physics setup. Run the suite twice. Both runs pass with the same assertions. Write stdout/stderr to `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/unit-tests-2.log`.\\n3. gating: Serve the real root HTML over localhost HTTP (WebGPU needs a secure context; `file://` must not be the launch path). If a headless browser is available (Playwright is expected), load the page twice and capture console plus DOM text to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`. Both runs must agree. Pass only if the visible product title is the chosen riff (not \\\"Mario Kart\\\", \\\"TODO\\\", or an empty title) and a menu control to start play is present, with no unexpected page error; the canvas drawing buffer matches its layout size when WebGPU initializes. If WebGPU or a secure context is unavailable, the page must show that WebGPU and a local server are required rather than a blank screen or a WebGL view. Do not take screenshots and do not grade pixels (TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review). If the browser cannot start for environmental reasons, save that failure output to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`; criteria 1–2 static checks plus the unit tests are then the accepted bar. A load that succeeds but is blank with no explanatory message is a product failure, not an environment fallback.\\n4. evidence: Note in `{SCRATCH}/stack-notes.md` the cel-shade technique, the animation technique, and which menu entries open cup, versus, time trial, and battle. Absence of this note alone does not fail the goal if steps 1–3 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- The full MK64 roster (16 courses, four cups, 50/100/150cc, mirror/extra mode), slipstreaming, snaking balance, and saved ghost replays.\\n- Online multiplayer, touch driving controls, desktop cursor menus, FPS targets, and rendering optimization.\\n- Nintendo-owned names, courses, music, manual text, or assets (the source name belongs in the filename and credits only).\\n- Screenshots, pixel/visual review, adversarial review passes, and subagents. TASK.md forbids them for this build.\\n- Inlining CDN libraries into the HTML when a pinned external URL is enough.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root is this folder (currently only `TASK.md`). New dev subfolder plus one root HTML file as named in criterion 1.\\n- Template token SOURCE (\\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\") is hyphenated to `Mario-Kart-64-for-N64` so the deliverable is one path segment. `{Slug}` is letters, digits, and hyphens only.\\n- CDN pins: `three@0.180` WebGPU entry and `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` (or the official Rapier 0.19.3 browser build). No other physics library and no WebGL fallback.\\n- Pure race, item, mode, attract, and input-mapping logic lives in modules the page bundle and the node tests both load. Course geometry, materials, audio buffers, and the HTML shell may stay in the dev folder until bundled.\\n- Original content scale for the slice: at least two race courses, one battle arena, and at least three characters. Not the retail course list.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep rules (input mapping including invert, kart handling, laps and rank, item roulette and effects, cup/versus/time-trial/battle transitions, attract timer) in pure functions with no DOM, WebGPU, or audio inside them so node tests call the real code. A thin shell owns the Rapier world, Three.js r180 WebGPU scene, cel materials, animation update, Web Audio triggers, gamepad polling, and split-view cameras, and calls those functions each frame. Bundle the shell and modules into the single root HTML; load Three and Rapier from pinned CDNs if easier. On `file://` or a missing WebGPU adapter, show the server/WebGPU requirement and stop. Pick the title by executing an RNG over the 50 riffs and write the list plus the pick into the HTML comment. Fill About/Credits from the same text.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Run an RNG over 50 original riff titles, record the pick, and create the dev subfolder plus the named root HTML shell.\\n- [ ] Implement pure input, race, item, mode, and attract logic, then the node tests in verification step 2.\\n- [ ] Add two courses, one battle arena, original characters, Rapier 0.19.3 kart collision, cel-shaded WebGPU scene, animation update, HUD, and Web Audio cues.\\n- [ ] Wire the 10-foot menu, invert-axis settings, 2/3/4-player splits, cup, versus, time trial, balloon battle, pause, attract mode, touch-only message, and About/Credits.\\n- [ ] Bundle to the single root HTML with r180 WebGPU and Rapier 0.19.3, no WebGL fallback, and a clear file:// / no-WebGPU message. Remove every TODO, placeholder, and stub.\\n- [ ] Run the unit suite twice and the localhost launch twice; write the logs under `{SCRATCH}`. Put Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment, the About screen, and the final reply.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md requires WebGPU with no fallback, and WebGPU needs a secure context plus an adapter. Headless Chromium here may lack a working adapter. Do not satisfy that by adding WebGL. Use the launch escape hatch in verification step 3 when the browser cannot start or cannot create an adapter; a blank successful load is still a failure.\\n- TASK.md forbids screenshots and visual review, which conflicts with a pixel-fill launch gate. The launch gate is DOM text, console errors, and canvas buffer size only.\\n- The filename template in TASK.md contains spaces (`GameTitle-SOURCE-...`, SOURCE = \\\"Mario Kart 64 for N64\\\"). This plan hyphenates those spaces so the path is one segment. A file that keeps the spaces but is otherwise identical still meets the template; either form is acceptable if the fixed suffix `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637.html` and the source token are present.\\n- The final chat reply is not in the repo. Verifiers confirm Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback in the HTML comment and About/Credits screen; the reply requirement is the implementer's and cannot be proven from files alone.\\n- Retail MK64 mode count and course count would make the slice unfinishable. Criterion 5 is the defining subset (cup of two courses, versus, time trial, one balloon arena, 8-kart field, full item classes). Anything beyond that is a non-goal, not a silent drop.\\n\",\"new_line\":1,\"context_before\":\"\",\"context_after\":\"\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"b8ebf91b-fc8e-452a-b881-cef2990618c9"}
