16,58,32,82,71,70,111,114,109,97,116,44,32,116,121,112,101,58,32,72,97,108,102,70,108,111,97,116,84,121,112,101,44,32,100,101,112,116,104,58,32,115,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,46,100,101,112,116,104,44,32,100,101,112,116,104,66,117,102,102,101,114,58,32,102,97,108,115,101,32,125,32,41,59,10,52,49,51,49,52,58,9,9,9,9,9,115,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,46,95,118,115,109,83,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,72,111,114,105,122,111,110,116,97,108,46,116,101,120,116,117,114,101,46,110,97,109,101,32,61,32,39,86,83,77,72,111,114,105,122,111,110,116,97,108,39,59,10,52,49,51,49,56,58,9,9,9,9,116,104,105,115,46,118,115,109,83,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,72,111,114,105,122,111,110,116,97,108,32,61,32,115,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,46,95,118,115,109,83,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,72,111,114,105,122,111,110,116,97,108,59,10,52,49,51,54,56,58,9,9,99,111,110,115,116,32,102,105,108,116,101,114,70,110,32,61,32,115,104,97,100,111,119,46,102,105,108,116,101,114,78,111,100,101,32,124,124,32,116,104,105,115,46,103,101,116,83,104,97,100,111,119,70,105,108,116,101,114,70,110,40,32,114,101,110,100,101,114,101,114,46,115,104,97,100,111,119,77,97,112,46,116,121,112,101,32,41,32,124,124,32,110,117,108,108,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":20,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/tmp/grok-goal-17adb0589bdc/implementer/three.webgpu.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":39810,\"content\":\"\\t\\tif ( light.castShadow !== true || frame.renderer.shadowMap.enabled === false ) {\"},{\"line_number\":41099,\"content\":\"\\t\\tthis.shadowMap = null;\"},{\"line_number\":41267,\"content\":\"\\t\\tconst shadowMap = builder.createRenderTarget( shadow.mapSize.width, shadow.mapSize.height );\"},{\"line_number\":41268,\"content\":\"\\t\\tshadowMap.texture.name = 'ShadowMap';\"},{\"line_number\":41269,\"content\":\"\\t\\tshadowMap.texture.type = shadow.mapType;\"},{\"line_number\":41270,\"content\":\"\\t\\tshadowMap.depthTexture = depthTexture;\"},{\"line_number\":41272,\"content\":\"\\t\\treturn { shadowMap, depthTexture };\"},{\"line_number\":41288,\"content\":\"\\t\\tconst shadowMapType = renderer.shadowMap.type;\"},{\"line_number\":41290,\"content\":\"\\t\\tconst { depthTexture, shadowMap } = this.setupRenderTarget( shadow, builder );\"},{\"line_number\":41296,\"content\":\"\\t\\tif ( shadowMapType === VSMShadowMap && shadow.isPointLightShadow !== true ) {\"},{\"line_number\":41300,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\tif ( shadowMap.depth > 1 ) {\"},{\"line_number\":41302,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\tif ( ! shadowMap._vsmShadowMapVertical ) {\"},{\"line_number\":41304,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tshadowMap._vsmShadowMapVertical = builder.createRenderTarget( shadow.mapSize.width, shadow.mapSize.height, { format: RGFormat, type: HalfFloatType, depth: shadowMap.depth, depthBuffer: false } );\"},{\"line_number\":41305,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tshadowMap._vsmShadowMapVertical.texture.name = 'VSMVertical';\"},{\"line_number\":41309,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\tthis.vsmShadowMapVertical = shadowMap._vsmShadowMapVertical;\"},{\"line_number\":41311,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\tif ( ! shadowMap._vsmShadowMapHorizontal ) {\"},{\"line_number\":41313,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tshadowMap._vsmShadowMapHorizontal = builder.createRenderTarget( shadow.mapSize.width, shadow.mapSize.height, { format: RGFormat, type: HalfFloatType, depth: shadowMap.depth, depthBuffer: false } );\"},{\"line_number\":41314,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\t\\tshadowMap._vsmShadowMapHorizontal.texture.name = 'VSMHorizontal';\"},{\"line_number\":41318,\"content\":\"\\t\\t\\t\\tthis.vsmShadowMapHorizontal = shadowMap._vsmShadowMapHorizontal;\"},{\"line_number\":41368,\"content\":\"\\t\\tconst filterFn = shadow.filterNode || this.getShadowFilterFn( renderer.shadowMap.type ) || null;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"294c00ff-3e1a-46b6-8368-4fbde50727a5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_10","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"WebGPURenderer supports setScissorTest and shadowMap.\n\nPlanning the game code modules: titles, credits, characters, courses, input, items, race simulation, modes, physics, and audio.\n\nAdding `src/scene.js` and `src/main.js` to the module plan. Now drafting the course sampling math: cumulative distances, position modulo length, heading, right vector, and elevation.\n\nImplementing the course sampling functions. `buildCourse` computes segment lengths and total distance. `sampleCourse` wraps distance modulo length and interpolates along the polyline.\n\nWorking through the course sampling math. Interpolating position along the segment and computing heading from the segment vector. Deriving the right vector by rotating that heading 90 degrees around the Y axis.\n\nVerifying the right vector math. The dot product with forward is zero, but the cross product against up yields a negative value, indicating a left-handed result. Recalculating the cross product to determine the correct orientation.\n\nThe correct right vector is `Y × forward`, which gives `(cos H, 0, -sin H)`. This places +X as the right direction when heading is zero.\n\nFinishing the course sampling function with slope calculation. Designing the first track, Cinder Loop, as a rounded rectangle with a sine hill and inward chicane. Planning procedural point generation for smooth curves. Item boxes will alternate laterally every ~50 meters. Starting on Lantern Causeway next.\n\nLantern Causeway will be a longer, narrower track with a hairpin and elevated bridge, using night colors. Balloon Yard is an arena instead of a loop: a 60×40 rectangle with circular and box obstacles, where battle mode uses free driving. The course data structure now distinguishes between circuit and arena kinds. Next up is the race state.\n\nDesigning the `createRace` factory. It accepts a course, entrants array, mode string, seed, and flags for item boxes and attract mode. Entrant counts vary by mode: eight for cup, one for trial, two to four for versus. Versus races will be filled with CPUs up to eight total to keep the field full. Split-screen versus is strictly human-only.\n\nBattle mode will use exactly 2–4 human players with no CPU fill. Each player starts with three balloons.\n\nPlanning four characters across three weight classes for the versus mode.\n\nFinalized the four characters: Pip (feather), Quill (light), Bramble (middle), and Holt (heavy). This gives four distinct stat profiles, exceeding the minimum three-class requirement. Now moving on to handling numbers for meters and seconds.\n\nTargeting 450–600 meter courses with an average speed of 18 m/s. A three-lap cup race should last about ninety seconds. Feather characters will accelerate faster than heavier ones. Now drafting the character stat constants.\n\nPip is defined as feather-weight with acceleration 22, top speed 23, and a red body. Quill is light-weight with acceleration 18, top speed 25, and a yellow body.\n\nBramble is middle-weight with acceleration 14, top speed 27, and a green body. Holt is heavy-weight with acceleration 10, top speed 30, and a blue body. Handle values are radians per second at full steer; Bramble’s 2.1 rad/s equals roughly 120 degrees per second. Now beginning the speed model.\n\nDrafting the `targetSpeed` function. It scales top speed by offroad and brownout states, shrinks it during mini-turbo or boost pulses, and caps it when a starting penalty is active. Braking input returns zero speed above one meter, or reverse speed at or below one meter.\n\nThe `targetSpeed` function now returns zero when neither accelerating nor braking, coasting with drag instead of full brake. The `integrateSpeed` function uses acceleration when chasing the target speed, deceleration during braking, or a default 8 m/s² otherwise; boost states snap faster. Offroad top speed is 0.58 times the onroad value, so Pip reaches roughly 13.3 m/s after two seconds of acceleration rather than 23 m/s.\n\nConfirmed the offroad speed gap: after two seconds of acceleration, onroad reaches roughly 23 m/s while offroad caps near 13 m/s. Now drafting the drift system with hop initiation and a two-stage charge threshold.\n\nThe drift update logic now checks that the hop button is held and the steer direction matches the drift direction. Charge time advances until it reaches the first or second stage thresholds. Releasing the button triggers a mini-turbo boost and a speed bonus based on the current drift stage.\n\nDrafting a test for the two-stage drift boost. The kart must reach 20 m/s before holding hop and steer to trigger stage two. Releasing the button should apply a mini-turbo and raise speed. During the hold, speed may drop.\n\nDrift release now clamps speed to top plus bonus, guaranteeing a net gain. A slight maintenance force will keep drift speed from dropping too low.\n\nThe rocket start countdown lasts 3.2 seconds with a 0.35-second early window. Human karts can press accelerate early to trigger an early start.\n\nThe countdown phase now transitions to racing once the timer expires. Karts that pressed early receive a 1.15-second start penalty and zero speed, while those who pressed late get a 0.7-second boost at eighty-five percent of top speed. CPUs in normal races will also receive a clean rocket start so the player must time their own launch to compete.\n\nAll CPUs will receive a rocket start boost so the field stays competitive. The player who early-presses gets left behind, while those who hit the window match CPU speed and those who do nothing remain slightly behind without penalty. Tests will compare two human races using the same kart index or a single entrant. During the countdown phase, kart positions are locked and integration is paused.\n\nAudio events such as countdown ticks, impacts, and item pickups will be recorded into a race events array for the shell to play. This keeps audio logic out of the pure simulation code.\n\nThe wall collision resolver now branches by course kind. For arena tracks it calls a dedicated arena resolver; for circuits it samples the track at each kart’s current progress and recomputes lateral position from the kart’s world coordinates.\n\nThe circuit wall resolver now clamps lateral position to the track half-width. When a kart exceeds that limit, it is pushed back inward and its speed drops by twenty-eight percent. Heading is also nudged slightly toward the center of the track.\n\nThe circuit wall resolver now sets an offroad flag when a kart drifts past the road edge but stays within the track boundary. The kart collision resolver is being drafted to check pairwise distances between active karts, using a small epsilon to prevent division by zero.\n\nThe kart collision resolver now resolves overlap by pushing each kart along the normal vector, weighted by their relative weights so the lighter kart moves more. Speed is exchanged between colliding karts based on their mass ratio and a 0.15 impulse factor.\n\nThe kart collision resolver now deducts 1.5 m/s from each kart’s speed along the separation vector to make the bump feel real. Aegis protection is implemented so the invincible kart does not slow down; instead, the other kart spins and loses a balloon in battle mode. Both karts are marked as bumped after a collision. A wall test will place a kart beyond the boundary at 20 m/s and step once.\n\nThe wall test will place a kart beyond the boundary while keeping its progress and heading aligned with the track, then step once. The kart should end up clamped inside the wall limit with speed reduced below twenty m/s. Cheats skip wall collisions entirely; test karts are not cheat karts.\n\nFor kart-kart collision testing, two karts will be placed at the same position with speed ten and one step taken. They must separate by at least their combined radii, or resolve to a minimum safe distance. After integration they may overlap briefly before the resolver pushes them apart. Kart radius is approximately 0.8.\n\nThe progress projection function searches a thirty-five-unit window around the kart’s current progress to find the closest track sample. It evaluates twenty-eight steps and keeps the absolute progress value unwrapped rather than modulo.\n\nThe integration step updates kart position using sine and cosine of heading multiplied by speed and delta time, then sets y to the sampled track height plus hop height. Progress advances by roughly speed times delta time when the heading aligns with the track. The lap logic is now being drafted.\n\nLap counting now uses `Math.floor(progress / course.length)` to compute the current lap, with a display cap of three. Karts begin at progress zero and finish after three full laps, so the start line doubles as the finish line. The staggered start grid logic is being drafted next.\n\nThe staggered start grid places karts in two columns, with lateral offsets of ±2.2 meters and progress values spaced 3.2 units apart. Karts beginning at negative progress remain on lap one until they cross the start line.\n\nThe lap logic is being corrected to match Mario Kart 64 behavior. Karts begin just before the start line, so crossing it at the end of their first lap marks lap two. This means the grid distance must be accounted for in the progress calculation.\n\nThe grid is positioned before the finish line, not after it. Karts begin just behind the start line so that crossing it at the end of their first lap marks lap two. The previous assumption that karts start at negative progress was incorrect.\n\nThe starting grid is placed ten meters behind the start line at progress negative ten. Karts cross the line immediately upon starting and remain on lap one until they return to it.\n\nThe lap display logic is now defined as a three-tier threshold: progress below the course length shows lap one, between one and two lengths shows lap two, and at or above three lengths shows lap three. Karts starting ten meters behind the line therefore complete three full laps plus that grid distance.\n\nThe rank update function is being drafted to sort karts by their current progress.\n\nThe rank function now sorts finished karts by finish time, then eliminated karts, then progress. The CPU AI samples the track fourteen units ahead plus a speed-based offset to compute a desired heading.\n\nThe CPU AI steers by wrapping the heading difference and clamping it to a range. It triggers a hop to start drifting when steering is strong and speed is high, then releases the hop during drift to charge for turbo. Item usage logic is also being added.\n\nThe CPU AI now triggers item usage when holding a boost, being ranked below fourth place, or having an opponent nearby. Cheats grant items inside the step function before input is processed. The items constants are being defined.\n\nItem constants are being defined with class IDs from the spec as keys and original display names. Weak, medium, and strong tiers are grouped by class. A `weightsForPlace` function is being drafted to assign item weights based on kart rank.\n\nThe `weightsForPlace` function now scales item probabilities by kart rank. The leader receives a 72% chance of a weak item and a 6% chance of a strong one, while the last-place kart gets only 14% weak and 62% strong. A `rollItem` helper samples from the weak, medium, or strong pools using these tier weights. The roulette system implementation is beginning.\n\nThe roulette system tracks a timer that accumulates each step. It stops early if the player presses useItem, otherwise it rolls once after 1.8 seconds. The displayed cycle is purely cosmetic and updates until the roll completes.\n\nItem boxes respawn after four seconds of cooldown once collected. A kart can only pick up a box when it is alive, the kart holds no item, and the kart is not in roulette.\n\nThe trial configuration disables item boxes and starts the kart with a held triSpark. The `useHeld` function is being drafted to handle item activation: shields trigger a trailing state on brake input, and a second press throws the item forward; spark and triSpark items grant a boost duration and minimum speed.\n\nThe `useHeld` function now decrements spark and triSpark charges and removes the item once exhausted. SparkStorm grants a 3.2-second storm effect with a pulse timer. Aegis grants a 2.6-second invincibility shield. Brownout applies a 3.5-second slowdown to all other unfinished karts. The pilfer implementation is beginning.\n\nThe pilfer item now targets the kart ahead or an opponent with a held item. It steals that item and removes any roulette or trailing state from the target. If no valid target exists, it either spins the target or discards the pilfer. Scatter spawns three slick hazards around the kart and consumes the item. Slick and phantom items are now being handled.\n\nThe trailing shield is a hazard-like entity that follows the kart and consumes on impact. It requires a brake input to activate, while firing projectiles happens immediately when use is pressed without brake. The next section covers projectile logic.\n\nProjectile definitions are being drafted. Ricochet moves at speed 42 straight ahead and bounces off arena walls. Homing moves at speed 36 toward the nearest opponent with greater progress, excluding the user. Crownseeker moves at speed 34 targeting the leading kart, or rank two if the user is first. All three use a hit radius of 1.1. On impact a victim spins for 0.9 seconds, loses 70% speed, and pops their balloon in battle mode. A crownseeker hit also strips the target’s held item and triggers a larger spin. If the victim carries a trailing shield, that shield is consumed instead.\n\nAegis hits are ignored by the victim. Slick and phantom hazards remain in the world; overlap triggers a spin, with phantom giving a longer one. Spark storm pulses every 0.45 seconds to grant a boost and minimum speed while active. Brownout halves the target's speed and is observable as a one-second speed drop compared to the user.\n\nA `slowMul` multiplier will be stored immediately on brownout. Aegis will set its own `aegisT` and the victim's `spinT` on collision. Spins will lock controls via `controlLock` or ignore steering while active.\n\nBattle mode gives each kart three balloons. Popping one triggers a 0.8-second spin and eliminates the kart if all balloons are gone. The winner is the last non-eliminated kart. A cup system is being defined with a two-course schedule and point values of ten, eight, six, five, four, three, two, and one.\n\nThe cup object now tracks a points bank, standings, and retry count. The `recordResult` function awards points based on the player's rank, advances the course index, and returns a retry or finished action.\n\n`recordResult` now returns a `continue` action for fourth-place results. A ten-second attract idle timer is being added to the session state, and the step function will advance the timer while the player remains on the title or main menu.\n\nThe attract mode logic is now complete. It activates after ten seconds of idle time on the title or main menu, and any input immediately exits attract back to the title screen. A touch gate helper is also being added to detect touch-capable devices.\n\nA touch-only warning message is being defined. The input module now inverts axis directions based on settings and applies an 18 percent dead zone to pad sticks.\n\nThe pad input mapping is now complete. Right trigger accelerates, left trigger brakes, and the right shoulder button triggers a hop. The left shoulder button activates items, while Y opens the camera, Start pauses, A confirms, and B goes back. Menu navigation uses the D-pad or left stick, and look direction reads from the right analog axes.\n\nTests are being drafted for `applyAxisInvert` to verify that only the requested axis is negated. They will also confirm that `steer` and `menuY` flip independently through `actionsFromPad`.\n\nKeyboard controls are being mapped to the same action shape. Player one will use WASD for steering, Space for hop, F for item, C for camera, Enter for pause, and Escape for pause. Player two will use arrow keys, Right Shift for hop, Right Ctrl for item, and Enter for pause. Player three will use IJKL for steering, U for hop, and O for item. Player four is still being assigned.\n\nPlayer four will use the numpad for steering, acceleration, braking, hop, and item. The keyboard menu now accepts any confirm key in addition to P1 controls. Mouse input is being drafted by scaling mouse deltas into a clamped axis pair.\n\nMouse input will not apply axis inversion, since that setting is gamepad-only. The drafted mouse actions map left click to accelerate, right click to brake, middle click to hop, and wheel up or right click to use an item. On the menu, left click serves as confirm and right click as back. The shell must prevent accidental menu steering while racing with a locked pointer.\n\nDuring races, keyboard and gamepad inputs merge into player zero while the mouse is locked to the pointer. On menus, pad and keyboard controls drive navigation instead of mouse movement, and the settings button handles touch input.\n\nThe physics module is being initialized with Rapier 3D. A `loadRapier` helper ensures the engine loads once, and `createRaceWorld` sets up a physics world with gravity at negative twenty-two on the Y axis.\n\nThe physics world is being populated with a large fixed ground plane and arena walls. A dynamic ball-shaped cone is created above the track drop point for the starting kart, configured with low restitution and non-sleeping behavior.\n\nThe physics module now exports `stepWorld` to advance the simulation and `bodyY` to read vertical positions. The game loop will step the world each frame and sync the cone mesh to its physics body. Circuit walls are placed every six meters along the track. A test verifies that the cone drops more than half a meter after thirty steps. The shell clears the race events array before each race.\n\nScene.js will create renderer-independent THREE objects for the course, karts, boxes, cone, and lights. It will expose an update function that receives delta time, total time, race state, and the cone's physics position.\n\nThe UI will be built entirely in main.js under a single #ui root. Screens include title, main menu, how to play, character select, course select, player count, settings, about, HUD, pause, results, and a touch banner. The flow starts at title, then moves to main menu, character select, and finally the first race course.\n\nVersus mode will use a shared character select screen where each player cycles their kart independently and confirms to start. Time Trial flows from course selection into character selection, then the race. Balloon Battle will first choose player count and characters, then load an arena. The how-to drive, settings, and about screens are also planned. Versus course selection will present two courses. Character selection will display four large cards.\n\nA simultaneous multiplayer picker was considered but rejected as too complex. Instead, a lineup screen will let each player cycle their kart character with the D-pad and confirm with Start. One controller can set all characters at once.\n\nThe HUD will display each player's position, lap count, speed in km/h, active item, and race standings. For split-screen play, four separate HUD clusters will be positioned in each viewport corner.\n\nA countdown overlay will display the race count-down label. Pause mode toggles via the Start button and offers Resume or Quit to title options. Results will list finish order, cup points with a Next course or Cup complete prompt, and time trial times against session bests. The attract HUD will show \"Demo reel — press anything\". Camera distance and height vary by mode: bumper mode uses 2.2 units back at 1.3 units high, far mode uses 9 units back at 2.6 units high, and normal mode uses 6.2 units back at 2.6 units high.\n\nCamera position is interpolated toward the target kart using an exponential decay curve. The camera will cycle through its modes on each button press. Split-screen viewports are being calculated for one to three players, with P1 assigned to the top half of the screen.\n\nThe title screen will feature a rotating cel-shaded kart on a podium, switching to an active race when attract mode begins. Item boxes will render as spinning cubes.\n\nItem boxes will use a bright emissive toon material or a saturated yellow cube. Drift sparks will be pooled small spheres toggled on and off, while boost flame will be a cone behind the kart scaled by boost level. Animation must run inside the render loop. The audio module is being drafted with an AudioContext factory that resumes suspended contexts.\n\nThe audio module will expose unlock, countdown, engine, item, and impact methods. Engine sounds will use one oscillator per local player, with frequency updated each frame after the first user gesture unlocks the context. Countdown beeps will be triggered by race events. Code will be kept compact and readable.\n\nThe build pipeline will bundle main.js into dist/game.js and wrap the HTML with build.mjs. Tests will import directly from src rather than the bundled output. I will scan for TODOs, FIXMEs, placeholders, and stubs before finalizing. A credits.js file will be drafted with honest feedback, and issue counts will be updated after testing.\n\nThe environment will be recorded as Grok CLI (headless) on Linux with an unknown browser. If Playwright launch checks succeed, the browser may be identified as Chromium headless. Code size targets are roughly 800 lines for main.js, 700 for scene.js, and 800 for race.js.\n\nDrift release timing is being checked against test expectations. The speed boost must apply after integration so the post-step value stays high. The stepKart sequence reads input, zeros steering during spin, starts drift on hop edge, and updates drift before integrating.\n\nThe kart step sequence is being finalized: update boost timers, compute the speed target including mini-turbo, integrate speed and position, then handle projection, walls, surface, items, and collisions. To satisfy the test that release speed must exceed charge speed, the speed value will be captured immediately before the release step.\n\nRocket start timing is being checked against test expectations. During the countdown phase karts remain stationary. After the GO signal each kart takes one acceleration step. The boost kart starts with 0.85 times its top speed and an active boost timer. The early kart starts at zero speed but carries a penalty that reduces its effective top speed to about 9.2. The none kart starts at zero speed with normal acceleration. After 0.4 seconds the boost kart should be near or at its top speed. The early kart should reach roughly 9.2. The none kart should reach about 11.\n\nThe boost kart should reach roughly 25 speed after one acceleration step. The early kart will use a reduced acceleration rate of 0.35 during its penalty so it lags far behind. Offroad mode caps top speed at 0.58, and karts must stay offroad by keeping heading aligned with the track while lateral position remains constant.\n\nA `placeOnCourse` helper will position karts on the track using sampled course geometry, lateral offset, and heading. It will be exported for use in grid spawning as well.\n\nThe lap test will place a kart at five units before the course end with high speed and acceleration, then step until its progress crosses the finish line.\n\nLap display will show two digits. A rank test will place one kart at high progress and another at low, step once, and verify the higher-positioned kart ranks first. Item tests require the racing phase, so the countdown can be skipped by stepping through it with no acceleration or by using a `beginRacing` helper.\n\nA `race.clock` will advance only during the racing phase. Item collection tests will place a kart on a box, step to consume it, and verify the box respawns after five seconds. Using an item during roulette will stop the spin and grant the held item. Position bias does not require a full race if `rollItem` is what the collection logic uses.\n\nThe item bias test will exercise `rollItem` directly and verify that `collectBox` delegates to it. A finish-order test will place eight karts at different progress levels, advance one past the finish line, step once, and confirm the rank order.\n\nSetting kart progress without position would be overwritten by projection, so `placeOnCourse` is required. The helper wraps progress modulo the course length while keeping the kart’s internal progress unwrapped. `projectProgress` searches within a ±35-unit window around the current progress, so it will not snap to a different lap.\n\nA hop visual effect will be added, though tests only exercise drift. Reverse driving will be implemented: holding brake at or below speed 1 targets reverse, and if both accelerate and brake are held, brake overrides acceleration. Steering will invert during reverse to match the original game. The offroad dirt shortcut is handled by lateral position alone.\n\nThe course will use a uniform roadHalf for predictable test behavior. A `loopPoints` generator is being drafted to produce the Cinder Loop as a bean-shaped track with wobble, stretch, and a hill.\n\nThe loop track will have a radius near 80 and a total length of roughly 440 meters. A second course, the Lantern, will use scale 96 with more wobble and a higher hill on a bridge section. Course points will be kept convex to avoid self-intersections that could destabilize projection. Item boxes will be placed eight times around the track at alternating lateral offsets of ±3. Arena obstacles will be implemented as walls inside `resolveArena`.\n\nThe arena collision logic clamps karts to the bounds and reduces speed by 30 percent on contact. Cylinder obstacles are handled with a simple distance check, pushing the kart back to the obstacle’s surface.\n\nBattle karts will spawn around the arena facing the center. The spec requires 2–4 players, so CPU opponents may be added.\n\nVersus mode will fill the grid to eight racers by adding CPU opponents. The UI will note that the remaining karts are driven by the derby. Versus differs from cup because it is a single race with any place, course selection, and two to four human players.\n\nTime trial mode will be solo with no rivals. The starting multi-boost is a held triSpark item, giving three charges at the start; the UI will display \"Three peppers are already in your pocket.\" Grand cup mode will use one human player and seven CPU opponents cycling through different characters.\n\nCPU opponents will be named Nib, Cinder, Pock, Lumen, Tad, Moss, Vesper, and Bruck. Each character maps to a stat class, and every kart—human or CPU—will display its character name so the player knows the weight.\n\nDuplicate character names are allowed in versus mode if players pick the same kart. CPU opponents will cycle through the remaining characters and repeat once the pool is exhausted.\n\nA `checkEnd` function will end battle races when only one kart remains alive, or end cup races when every human player has finished.\n\nAttract mode will run with all CPU karts. A cheat flag prevents them from getting stuck. The race ends when every kart finishes or the clock exceeds 180 seconds. When no humans are present, the demo restarts after the leading CPU kart crosses the finish line.\n\nA stuck-CPU recovery will nudge heading toward the next sampled course point and restore speed when a kart remains nearly stopped for over 1.5 seconds without spinning. Item box visuals will show a respawn animation, and the roulette UI will cycle through item names on the held slot.\n\nA settings screen will offer two toggles for inverting horizontal and vertical controls. The focused toggle flips on left, right, or confirm input and persists to session.settings for application at gamepad read time. The package.json will be corrected to set type module, and node_modules will remain in the dev folder without being bundled into the deliverable HTML.\n\nThe build script will externalize Three.js and Rapier via CDN imports. The compat ESM build inlines its WASM as base64, so it should load correctly from the CDN. The exact WebGPU secure context error message is \"WebGPU and a local server are required\".\n\nThe UI will use a canvas with id \"view\" and a title element with id \"product-title\". A button labeled \"Start\" will sit on the title screen to launch play. The first screen is the title, so the Start button itself satisfies the criterion for a menu control that begins racing.\n\nThe title screen will display the main menu buttons directly, so Grand Cup, Versus, Time Trial, and Balloon Battle are immediately visible alongside the product title. The attract idle timer begins as soon as the title screen appears. If WebGPU is unavailable, the gate banner overlays the title without hiding it, and the render loop stays paused.\n\nA meta viewport tag will be added. The UI targets 10-foot readability with large text, a `.sel` focus state, high contrast, and safe-area padding; the touch message appears as a banner. A full-screen overlay layer will ignore pointer events except on buttons that need interaction, allowing mouse users to navigate menus by clicking the focused item or any menu button.\n\nA test will verify lap increments and rank ordering across an eight-kart field by placing karts at staggered progress values, confirming ranks match, then advancing the leader past a lap boundary. Collision response will be observable through speed and position checks. Before writing all files, a quick Rapier validation script will confirm the physics engine loads correctly.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"