olorSpace\\\",\\\"NeutralToneMapping\\\",\\\"ACESFilmicToneMapping\\\",\\\"NoToneMapping\\\",\\\"DoubleSide\\\",\\\"FrontSide\\\",\\\"BackSide\\\",\\\"CylinderGeometry\\\",\\\"SphereGeometry\\\",\\\"BoxGeometry\\\",\\\"PlaneGeometry\\\",\\\"CircleGeometry\\\",\\\"RingGeometry\\\"]) {\\n  console.log(n, e.includes(n));\\n}\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Check lights, fog, and camera exports\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/terminal/call-c7ccc4d9-c655-44e9-b275-618dfd93bcd2-21.log\",\"total_bytes\":1181}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"d0852173-83fa-4a70-9c00-ad3ef6d5bf23"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_15","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Fog is not exported, but the scene can still be set.\n\nFog will be skipped. The scene is ready to build. Shadow maps are confirmed working. Source files will be written thoroughly. A modulo bug in projectProgress was spotted.\n\nThe modulo bug is resolved. The search window correctly wraps around negative progress values. Heading continuity and right vector math are verified.\n\nThe right vector formula is confirmed correct for a right-handed coordinate system. Kart integration is next.\n\nKart physics are being defined. Position updates use heading and speed. CPU steering clamps heading differences to a 0.55 rad limit. A wrapAngle helper normalizes angles. Drift logic adds lateral sliding and faster heading changes.\n\nDrift steering will override normal steer integration to prevent double application. The hop animation uses a sine curve for vertical offset. Start grid positions are calculated with negative progress and lateral offsets per row and column. Item boxes are being added to the circuit.\n\nItem boxes are placed every 12.5% of the course length with alternating lateral offsets. Collection radius is 2.2 units. A kart cannot collect an item while holding one, in roulette mode, or trailing. Respawn timer is five seconds. The race uses a seeded mulberry32 RNG.\n\nThe mulberry32 RNG implementation is complete. Events will be capped at fifty entries to prevent unbounded growth. race.js will serve as the core module, with modes.js handling cup, session, attract, touch, and trial best logic. Trial times are stored in the session object so they survive retries. A splitLayout helper was considered but deemed unnecessary.\n\nA separate `src/ui.js` module will handle DOM creation and updates, keeping `main.js` focused on the render loop. The game flow state will live in `session.screen` and cover title, help, settings, about, player selection, course selection, characters, race, pause, results, and attract modes.\n\nsession.pending will store the active mode, course ID, player count, and selected characters. The title menu is being defined with six options: Grand Cup, Versus, Time Trial, Balloon Battle, How to Drive, and Settings.\n\nThe title menu now includes an About option. Menu navigation is mapped to gamepad D-pad buttons 12 and 13, with up yielding -1 and down yielding +1. Stick input uses the sign of the Y axis. Edge detection prevents rapid menu skipping; the first movement registers after 0.35 seconds, then repeats every 0.18 seconds.\n\nInput edge detection is simplified to require a release before re-triggering. Confirming on the title menu starts the focused item, while confirming in character selection begins the race once all slots are filled. The pause menu will include Resume and Quit, and the results screen will offer Continue. A drift test case is being designed with a 1.4-second hold to verify charge accumulation.\n\nDrift charge thresholds are set at 0.55 and 1.15 seconds, with a 1.4-second hold to be safe. Turbo fires on the release frame once charge stops, without requiring steer input there. Acceleration is held during the test so speed does not drop. Mini-turbo speed will be verified by comparing kart speed on the last charge frame against the release frame.\n\nThe mini-turbo speed test is being refined. Boost sets kart.speed to the greater of its current value or top speed plus bonus, but the integration target remains top speed plus seven. This means the kart will coast downward from the boosted value toward the lower target.\n\nThe mini-turbo integration target is raised to top speed plus six times the drift stage so the boost matches or exceeds the jump. A `runStart` test helper is being drafted to simulate rocket starts by stepping the countdown and pressing acceleration at a chosen moment.\n\nThe `runStart` test helper is now defined. It steps the countdown and applies acceleration at a chosen moment, then races for half a second. The API distinguishes three start phases: early acceleration before the window, boost acceleration inside the window, and no acceleration during countdown. A latch prevents early and boost triggers from overlapping.\n\nCPU karts receive a start boost when the countdown ends if they did not press early. Attract mode gives them a larger boost. Human drivers who do not press get neither boost nor penalty. The audio countdown will emit events at 3, 2, and 1 seconds.\n\nA countdownLabel helper maps the remaining time to '3', '2', '1', or 'GO'. Events fire only when the label changes, preventing duplicate emissions. The HUD will display this large countdown text during the start sequence. A speedometer and position ordinal will also be added to the HUD.\n\nAn `ordinal` helper is defined for position labels. A dev `package.json` will be added, and tests will be comprehensive. Scene performance is acceptable with eight karts, outline shells, wheels, and forty trees. Track geometry will use a custom `BufferGeometry` with positions, normals, and colors, rendered with `MeshToonNodeMaterial` vertex colors.\n\nThe track will use a simpler mesh strategy. The road will be a single solid color. Edge strips will be separate thin ribbons with alternating red and white materials. Walls, offroad shoulders, and the infield plane will each use distinct colors. A `ribbon` helper is being drafted to build these strip meshes.\n\nThe ribbon helper now samples the course at fixed intervals to build left and right vertices for each segment. It uses a half-width offset along the right vector and a uniform upward normal. Indices will wrap around for closed-loop connectivity.\n\nWalls will be vertical ribbons rising 1.6 meters from the road edge on both sides, using a darker material. The start line is a white box spanning the track at progress zero. Item boxes are built as groups containing a cube and a smaller inner cube that rotates during animation. A kart mesh factory is being drafted to assemble each kart from a toon-colored body box positioned at y = 0.45 with shadow casting enabled.\n\nThe kart mesh factory now adds wheel meshes positioned at the four corners of the body with axle rotation. It also adds a driver group to the kart assembly.\n\nThe kart mesh factory now adds a spherical head and a box torso to the driver group, positioning them at y = 0.95 and y = 0.68. The driver group is offset to y = 0.15 and attached to the kart. Wheel spin will rotate around the x-axis to avoid gimbal lock.\n\nEach wheel is parented in a group so the group rotates around x for spin while the mesh keeps its z-axis cylinder orientation. The animateWorld function updates kart positions and headings from physics state, accumulates wheel spin from speed times delta time, and smoothly interpolates the driver's lean angle toward the inverse of the steer input.\n\nThe kart animation now adds a vertical bob and hop to the driver group, with bob amplitude scaling with speed. Item boxes rotate around both axes during animation. Steer input is stored on each kart object. Projectiles and hazards are pooled meshes hidden when unused, updated from physics positions. Cone meshes are positioned directly from physics state.\n\nLighting will use ambient 0.55, a hemisphere light, and a directional light with shadows. The shadow camera orthographic size is set to about 120, with a 2048×2048 map and bias of -0.001. Meshes cast and receive shadows. A large sky dome with a BackSide basic material will provide the cel-colored background.\n\nCourse color palettes are finalized for three environments. Cinder uses late-afternoon tones with amber background, dark road, tan shoulders, green grass, and brown walls. Lantern uses a night palette with dark blue background, slate road, charcoal shoulders, deep green grass, and golden lantern walls. Balloon yard uses noon colors with sky-blue background, tan floor, and brown walls. Point lights will be added along the lantern course to create extra toon bands.\n\nA titles.js module will export fifty title candidates, a fixed index of 21, the game title, and the slug. The HTML comment will record the Node.js crypto randomInt result that selected index 21.\n\nA copy module will hold original UI strings for consistency. The race step function signature is being drafted to accept a Race object, an aligned array of inputs indexed by kart index, and a delta time value. Missing or null entries will default to neutral actions for human karts and AI behavior for CPU karts. A neutralAction helper is also being defined.\n\nThe neutral action returns a default input object with zero steering and all buttons released. Delta time is clamped to 0.05 seconds to prevent simulation explosions from large time steps, though this may slow lap tests that attempt to skip ahead; attract mode tests will use stepSession instead.\n\nThe delta-time clamp is being removed from `stepRace`. A fixed 1/60-second substep loop will handle large spikes internally, so tests can pass larger values like 0.5 or 1.4 without breaking attract mode.\n\nInputs are held constant across substeps. The hop edge fires only on the first substep when the previous latch was false. The useItem button uses a latch to trigger once per stepRace call even if the key is held, preventing multiple charges from firing in one frame.\n\nCamera and pause inputs are shell-level edges, not simulation events. Item use will be blocked during the countdown. The substep loop caps at 180 steps per call, allowing up to three seconds of simulation time in a single stepRace invocation.\n\n`createRace` seeds a `mulberry32` RNG and maps entrants into kart states. For non-arena courses, karts are placed in a grid with staggered rows and alternating left or right offsets, each assigned a rank. Arena mode will use a different placement strategy.\n\nArena karts spawn at the course's spawn points in a rotating cycle. The race state initializes with a countdown phase, a 3.4-second timer, and a '3' label. Item boxes are disabled for trial mode unless explicitly requested, and triSpark is provided there.\n\nmakeKartState now initializes each kart with position, heading, speed, steer, progress, lateral offset, display lap, rank, and flags for finished, eliminated, offroad, and hop state. Trial mode gives the first kart a triSpark with three charges and removes all item boxes.\n\nmakeKartState now initializes every kart with full drift, mini-turbo, boost, storm, aegis, brownout, and spin timers. It also adds start-line latches, held items, roulette state, and item-lock flags. Kart radius is derived from weight plus 0.85. CPU karts in attract mode cheat; normal races do not.\n\nThe shell will create attract races with eight CPU entrants and the attract flag set to true. A 0.4-second item lock prevents re-triggering after use, while a separate itemLock field handles cheat auto-grant. Trailing shield collision is being implemented by checking nearby karts against a point offset 1.6 units behind the kart's heading.\n\nThe trailing shield collision now inflicts a 'shield' damage type on the target kart and consumes the shield. The `inflict` function adds an aegis-bump chain reaction: if the victim has an active aegis timer, it retaliates against the attacker. It also destroys any trailing shield held by the victim when they are hit by non-shield attacks.\n\nThe `inflict` function now records an impact event at the victim's position. It applies spin timers, reduces speed to 25 percent, clears drifting, pops balloons in battle mode, and strips held items for crownseeker hits. The `stepProjectiles` function is being drafted to handle homing and crownseeker projectiles by selecting targets.\n\nHoming and crownseeker projectiles now steer toward their target using atan2, capped at 2.4 or 3.2 radians per second depending on kind. They advance forward by speed times delta time and decay lifetime. Ricochet projectiles bounce off walls. The step then iterates over karts to check for collisions, skipping the owner, finished, and eliminated karts.\n\nProjectile collisions are detected when the distance between a kart and a projectile is less than the kart radius plus 0.45. The hit inflicts damage on the target kart, kills the projectile, and breaks out of the loop. Projectiles are then filtered to remove those with expired lifetime or too many bounces.\n\nRicochet physics are being implemented for track and arena courses. On tracks, the heading reflects across the wall normal based on lateral offset from the nearest sample point. On arenas, ricochets bounce off the bounds.\n\nThe ricochet reflection formula is now defined: it reflects the projectile's velocity vector across the wall normal using a dot product, then returns the new heading. A homing projectile will target the kart with the highest progress ahead of the user, choosing between closest angle and nearest distance.\n\nHoming targets default to the nearest kart if no kart outranks the user. Crownseeker targets the kart with rank 1, or rank 2 if the user holds first place. Projectiles spawn 1.8 units ahead of their owner at the same heading, with ricochets moving at speed 46 and other kinds at speed 38 for six seconds. The hazard spawn function places objects behind each kart in a staggered row.\n\nHazard spawn positions are calculated using the kart's heading and a staggered offset behind it. Slicks will drop behind the kart, while phantom boxes will be thrown forward as traps. After spawning, hazards remain unarmored for 0.25 seconds so the owner cannot hit them immediately; they then become active for all karts except the one that dropped them.\n\nHazard hits spin the target kart, with phantom boxes giving longer spins. The owner is protected from their own hazards for 0.8 seconds. Scatter spawns three slicks, and spark grants immediate speed boost. A test helper will step the race through countdown to racing phase. Offroad tests will place a kart offroad, zero its speed, and accelerate it for two seconds.\n\nPlacing a kart offroad during racing is safe because the next step integrates forward. The lateral offset is preserved after projection onto the centerline, so a kart placed 9 meters outside the road half-width remains offroad even on a curved oval.\n\nA unit test will verify that on-road speed exceeds off-road speed after identical elapsed time, using two separate races to avoid kart collisions. An eight-kart race will test ranking. Wall and kart collision tests must run during the racing phase, since the countdown does not integrate physics. Reverse speed sign is not required. The cup test does not need a full race.\n\nCup tests verify that a score of 4 continues the race, a score of 5 retries with index reset to zero, and a second continue finishes the race. A battle test will start a two-player arena match, confirm each kart has three balloons, and simulate ricochet usage to hit an opponent.\n\nThe battle test will place two karts facing each other three meters apart. One kart holds a ricochet with one charge. The test triggers the item and steps the race for forty frames. The projectile travels 2.3 meters per step, so it should hit within one step. Each hit pops a balloon. Three hits eliminate the opponent.\n\nThe use latch resets automatically after each step, so karts can re-hold and re-use items without delay. An aegis test will place two karts in overlapping positions, activate the shield, and verify that only the opponent receives a spin timer while the user remains unaffected.\n\nBrownout will not boost the user. It applies a slowdown to the target kart and clears any active boost from the user. Pilfer will steal the victim's held item and give it to the user. Crownseeker targets the kart with the highest progress ahead of the user, so the leader is placed just ahead for a quick hit.\n\nSpark storm tests will verify that stormT is active and speed exceeds a coasting baseline after one second. Tri spark charges three, so a trial mode can test the full sequence from two to one charge. Roulette bias is checked by running two identical random generators against weak-item thresholds and asserting the weak rate is at least twenty percent higher.\n\nEach rollItem call consumes two random values, so separate generators seeded identically still produce the same sequence. An optional test will verify that collect uses item weights. The attract-mode test checks that idle time accumulates to exactly ten seconds, triggering the mode and returning to the title screen.\n\nA session test will step the title screen for exactly ten seconds to trigger attract mode and verify input clears. Another test will confirm that idle time does not accumulate while the race screen is active. A pad-input test will check that inverting the X axis negates the steer value.\n\nVertical inversion flips the menuY sign while leaving steer unchanged. Horizontal inversion leaves menuY untouched.\n\nA critical gameplay test will verify that an early start is slower than a boost. The physics test is asynchronous and will be run twice. The shell main.js is structured to avoid throwing when DOM APIs are missing. Import boundaries are carefully isolated so tests never import scene, physics, or audio directly.\n\nThe WebGPURenderer initialization requires the canvas to be in the DOM and may throw asynchronously. The main entry point will check navigator.gpu and isSecureContext before attempting renderer setup, and it will display a gate if the browser lacks WebGPU support or runs over the file protocol.\n\nThe resize handler updates the renderer and camera to match the canvas client dimensions. It sets the pixel ratio to one so the buffer aligns with the layout. The CSS removes body margins and sets the background to a dark gray.\n\nThe CSS fixes the canvas to the viewport with full width and height. The render loop limits delta time to 0.05 seconds, updates the scene, and draws each view with scissor clipping. Whether to clear the renderer between views is still undecided.\n\nThe renderer will clear each frame automatically while scissor clipping is enabled, so the canvas buffer stays valid even when the menu screen is blank. A static title diorama showing a podium will be rendered continuously to keep the canvas visually populated.\n\nA 2D minimap canvas will be added to the HUD. The fieldEntrants function maps human character IDs into player entrants with names and indices, returning them directly for battle and trial modes.\n\nThe CPU entrant generator tracks used characters in a Set. It cycles through the pool and repeats names with an index suffix when eight karts are needed but only four human characters are provided.\n\nThe attract-mode loop will cycle through two courses. It creates a new eight-kart cup race with infinite items and cheat enabled for every kart, then steps the race forward while the camera follows kart zero.\n\nAny input during attract mode will exit it and destroy the attract race. Stick flicks below the deadzone are ignored as noise. Invert settings are stored in session state and read each frame when polling pads. Settings and trial best scores persist to localStorage, with errors silently ignored.\n\nThe trialBest object will store per-course seconds keyed by course name. A quit from pause returns directly to the title screen without extra confirmation dialogs. The driving instructions will be playful and concise, mapping each button to its specific action.\n\nController labels will use plain English names like \"Right trigger\" instead of trademark button glyphs. Keyboard controls will appear in smaller text on a single line beneath the controller mapping. A touch-gate banner will prompt for a controller or keyboard and include a settings button. The about screen will display the full Source Information, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback sections.\n\nThe build script will import titles.js and credits.js to generate HTML comment metadata. Credits will be updated with real test counts after the suite runs. The workflow is: write source and tests, run and fix, bundle, launch, update credits, run twice for logs, then finalize.\n\nI will not exclude node_modules from the TODO search. The verification only checks the shipped page and bundled sources, which are our own src files, so third-party dependencies should not affect the result.\n\nI will avoid input placeholders entirely to satisfy the no-placeholders rule. The physics module will import only rapier and accept course objects directly, keeping it free of three.js dependencies. Wall colliders will be many bodies.\n\nThirty physics steps with roughly one hundred colliders will keep tests fast. The arena will use four boundary cuboids plus obstacles. A cone drop test will assert the object falls more than half a unit in thirty frames, since gravity at negative twenty-two drops it about two and a half units in half a second.\n\nThe physics test will use a cone as a loose bumper ball that karts can collide with, since the world steps and course walls are already present. The cone position will be read directly from the race state to verify collisions.\n\nThe shell will copy the cone’s world translation into race.conePos after each physics step so the game can detect collisions. If a test omits this field, the check is skipped. To make the cone a real gameplay obstacle, the shell will apply an impulse to the cone body when the kart hits it.\n\nItem boxes will be static positions rather than physics bodies so collection is deterministic. The full file list is now set: fifteen source modules, a game test file, and the build script. A lightweight Node HTTP server is being considered for local testing.\n\nNode.js `fs` will be used for launch tests. Playwright 1.63 is installed but its import path still needs confirmation. The gradient map uses a four-pixel DataTexture with nearest-neighbor filtering.\n\nThe gradient texture will use nearest-neighbor filtering and no color space. Vertex colors are skipped in favor of material colors. The scene builder will accept the course and entrants as arguments. Old geometries will be disposed between races to prevent memory leaks.\n\nThe animation system will use procedural transforms in the render loop for wheel spin, driver lean, item-crate tumble, and drift sparks. Six small trailing spheres will form a drifting spark trail that cycles offset each frame. Additional vertical-slice polish includes a countdown lock, results screen with places and times, cup standings, session best on trial, pause, split-screen borders via CSS overlays, item roulette names, speed, lap, and position HUD, plus audio.\n\nThe minimap will be a 180×180 canvas drawing the course polyline or arena rectangle with kart dots, updated each frame. One minimap per human player will sit in their HUD corner. Ordinal and HUD text elements will be placed in the initial HTML so they are present before the deferred module loads.\n\nThe initial HTML will contain a title heading and a start button so the page remains usable if JavaScript fails. The menu will be built synchronously at module load, before any async WebGPU initialization, ensuring the title and button are present immediately.\n\nThe main entry point will call `mountUI()` synchronously to render the title and menu before any async boot work begins. If boot throws, the error message will appear in the gate element and be logged to the console. The expected missing-GPU path will avoid `console.error` so the test sees no unexpected page error.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4f1b5b83-b839-4edc-ba28-65cf90d961cf-22","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/node_modules/@dimforge/rapier3d-compat/init.d.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":616,"output_tokens":13851,"cache_read_input_tokens":83840,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"9676356d-0ff9-4639-82d5-7b5883bab5e6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4f1b5b83-b839-4edc-ba28-65cf90d961cf-22","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * Initializes RAPIER.\\n * Has to be called and awaited before using any library methods.\\n */\\nexport declare function init(): Promise<void>;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * Initializes RAPIER.\\n * Has to be called and awaited before using any library methods.\\n */\\nexport declare function init(): Promise<void>;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/node_modules/@dimforge/rapier3d-compat/init.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * Initializes RAPIER.\\n * Has to be called and awaited before using any library methods.\\n */\\nexport declare function init(): Promise<void>;\\n\",\"total_lines\":5}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"b353d90f-a895-4af7-9ac8-ea193c36420b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_16","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The warning likely comes from wasm-bindgen internals when init() is called without arguments.\n\nThe warning is harmless. I will not fix it unless it becomes a console error. Now I am checking the rapier.mjs source.\n\nThe rapier.mjs source is minified, so I will skip it. I am now writing the main game code across multiple files. I verified that substepping correctly re-reads inputs each step and that CPU input updates properly. I also decided to export `placeKart` from courses.js.\n\n`placeKart` now computes kart position from sampled course data and heading. Courses are built at module load so they are ready immediately. I am designing the lantern and cinder obstacles as stadium shapes to avoid self-intersection.\n\nI am now writing the `stadiumPoints` helper to generate obstacle vertices parametrically. The shape will have straight sides parallel to Z and rounded end caps, with segment counts split between the caps and the sides.\n\nI am tracing the obstacle vertices counterclockwise from the south straight. The bottom edge runs from west to east at z = -radius, and the east end cap is being generated next.\n\nThe `stadiumPoints` helper is now complete. It traces the obstacle vertices counterclockwise through the bottom straight, east cap, top straight, and west cap. I am double-checking the east cap angle mapping to ensure the rounded end connects properly.\n\nI confirmed the stadium cap angles avoid duplicate vertices at junctions. The cinder obstacle will use a hill function to create a rise on its east cap, while the lantern obstacle will use one for a bridge on its north straight.\n\nI finalized the obstacle dimensions. The cinder stadium is 140 m long with a 40 m radius, and the lantern is 160 m long with a 28 m radius. I set side segments to 28 and cap segments to 24 for roughly 5 m spacing. The cinder obstacle uses a 120 m straight and 36 m radius, while the lantern uses a 160 m straight and 28 m radius. Road half-widths are 8 m and 7 m, and wall half-widths are 12 m and 10.5 m. The start line will sit at the south straight midpoint.\n\nThe bottom straight runs west to east, giving heading π/2 and forward direction (1, 0). Grid points behind the start line sample backward along the loop toward the west cap. Item boxes will sit at 0.5 spacing from the start line. I am now defining the Balloon Yard arena with a central cylinder and a box obstacle.\n\nThe Balloon Yard arena now includes four more obstacles: a box at (16, 8), two small cylinders at (-18, 12) and (18, -12), and a box at (0, 16). Four spawn points are placed around the center facing inward. Elimination mode is the only battle mode; boost panels are skipped.\n\nI am creating a shared math.js module to hold clamp, wrapAngle, lerp, and mulberry32 helpers. I am also investigating a lap counter bug where negative progress interacts with the floor function at the finish line.\n\nI defined `lapForProgress` to return lap 1 for progress below the course length, lap 2 between one and two lengths, and lap 3 once past two lengths. The finish line is reached at three times the course length. I verified that negative progress correctly shows lap 1, and crossing the length boundary advances the display to lap 2. Rank ordering will place karts with higher progress ahead of those with lower progress, and finished karts will outrank any kart still racing.\n\nI verified the finish-line logic by testing two karts: one at 3×length+5 and one at length. The first finishes immediately, while the second remains racing. I confirmed that finishTime is set from the clock at the start of each racing step, and that updateRanks runs after the finish check.\n\nThe speed HUD will display integer km/h rounded from the kart's velocity. UI strings are being reviewed to avoid Nintendo names, with the exception of the required about-screen citation for Mario Kart 64. The about panel will be scrollable and use a large font to fit the full feedback text.\n\nTesting feedback placeholders will use \"0\" and be updated after testing. The required questions about issues caught, critical bugs, and test help will be answered honestly once results are known. Engine and prompt feedback can now be written from the current experience.\n\nI am deciding whether \"the engine\" in the feedback section refers to the game engine or the Grok agent. I am drafting honest answers to the six required questions about likes, stuck points, hardest parts, and desired changes.\n\nI resolved that \"the engine\" refers to the Three.js r180 WebGPU and Rapier physics stack, not the LLM harness. The prompt structure and the question about the hardest part confirm this interpretation.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3e984f01-1ec1-4f36-94f4-acd86e41bf2d-23","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/package.json"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":13996,"output_tokens":3101,"cache_read_input_tokens":84352,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"d1cdd44e-7eea-40c5-b760-37e2646a1fca"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3e984f01-1ec1-4f36-94f4-acd86e41bf2d-23","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"kartesian-derby-mario-kart-64-for-n64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"main\\\": \\\"index.js\\\",\\n  \\\"directories\\\": {\\n    \\\"test\\\": \\\"test\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n10→    \\\"test\\\": \\\"echo \\\\\\\"Error: no test specified\\\\\\\" && exit 1\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"keywords\\\": [],\\n  \\\"author\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"license\\\": \\\"ISC\\\",\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"commonjs\\\",\\n  \\\"dependencies\\\": {\\n    \\\"@dimforge/rapier3d-compat\\\": \\\"^0.19.3\\\",\\n    \\\"esbuild\\\": \\\"^0.25.0\\\"\\n  }\\n20→}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"kartesian-derby-mario-kart-64-for-n64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"main\\\": \\\"index.js\\\",\\n  \\\"directories\\\": {\\n    \\\"test\\\": \\\"test\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n10→    \\\"test\\\": \\\"echo \\\\\\\"Error: no test specified\\\\\\\" && exit 1\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"keywords\\\": [],\\n  \\\"author\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"license\\\": \\\"ISC\\\",\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"commonjs\\\",\\n  \\\"dependencies\\\": {\\n    \\\"@dimforge/rapier3d-compat\\\": \\\"^0.19.3\\\",\\n    \\\"esbuild\\\": \\\"^0.25.0\\\"\\n  }\\n20→}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/package.json\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"kartesian-derby-mario-kart-64-for-n64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"main\\\": \\\"index.js\\\",\\n  \\\"directories\\\": {\\n    \\\"test\\\": \\\"test\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"scripts\\\": {\\n    \\\"test\\\": \\\"echo \\\\\\\"Error: no test specified\\\\\\\" && exit 1\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"keywords\\\": [],\\n  \\\"author\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"license\\\": \\\"ISC\\\",\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"commonjs\\\",\\n  \\\"dependencies\\\": {\\n    \\\"@dimforge/rapier3d-compat\\\": \\\"^0.19.3\\\",\\n    \\\"esbuild\\\": \\\"^0.25.0\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":20}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"5a5131b5-e663-4c21-a0ca-075c4970b27d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_17","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Pinning rapier to version 0.19.3 and setting the type module.\n\nWriting all source files now. Credits will be a template function for dynamic dates and test stats. Adding `freshInputs(n)` for tests. Checking race.js length and correctness during implementation.\n\nFleshing out the item definitions with display names, tiers, and effect flags.\n\nFinalizing the remaining item definitions with display names, tiers, and effect flags. Roulette grants will default to one charge unless a definition specifies otherwise. The three shield classes that trail behind are ricochet, homing, and slick.\n\nHoming trails will appear as a red shell behind the kart. Using an item without the brake button throws it forward if already trailing. Slick only spawns its hazard when thrown or consumed, since the trail itself is the hazard. The releaseHeld function handles throwing by clearing the held state and spawning the appropriate projectile or hazard.\n\ngrantItem will set item charges. collectBox checks that the kart is not already holding an item, racing, or finished before spawning a 1.6-second roulette timer. The distance check runs in step using the box world position. finishRoulette stops the roulette and finalizes the result.\n\nfinishRoulette resolves the item by calling rollItem with the kart's rank and race length, then stores it in held state with charges. An early useItem press during roulette stops the spin immediately. The cosmetic cycle does not affect the result; bias tests call rollItem directly. A box world position helper is added to race using sampleCourse. CPUs exit roulette after 0.4 seconds to avoid stalling, while players may wait for the full duration or press use.\n\nCPU item usage is now defined. Spark types are consumed immediately. Projectiles fire when a kart is within 30 meters ahead. Brownout and crownseeker activate only if the CPU is not in first place. Shields trail behind a nearby kart or throw forward if one is ahead. Pilfer steals an item from a kart ahead.\n\nA demo attract mode grants the next item from a showcase list every 1.2 seconds while idle. The kart waits 0.3 seconds for the roulette HUD to cycle before using it.\n\nThe showcase list cycles through all twelve item types. Kart drifting state will drive the renderer. Scene.js will stay compact while remaining visually rich. Point lights will be placed along the lantern course and fewer for other layouts. Boxes will form a grandstand near the start, and trees with cylinder trunks and sphere tops will surround the outside.\n\nTree placement will sample every 20 meters outside the wall, skip positions too close to the start, and vary scale by index. Rocks are squashed spheres scaled to 0.6 on the Y axis. Two poles and a box form the start banner. The infield grass is a flat plane at y = -0.15; the track rises above it, with dirt shoulder ribbons between roadHalf and wallHalf.\n\nGaps under elevated track sections will show the underside. A second ribbon at y minus 0.4 or floating pillars with cylinders from ground to elevation will close them. Bridge pillars will sit at samples where the road rises above one unit. The track builder in scene.js is being written now. Ribbon normals will stay mostly upward for flat terrain, but may derive from tangent and right vectors on hills. Toon lighting will still create visible bands from directional light.\n\nFlat track shading is acceptable because kart and prop geometry carries proper normals for cel bands. The toon material will use a dark hex color. Outlines will be drawn with a scaled-backside child mesh.\n\nThe outline mesh is a child of the kart chassis, scaled to 1.05 so it traces the inverse hull. Box and sphere geometries are centered at origin, keeping the scaling correct. Wheels will be excluded from outlines to avoid visual clutter.\n\nDriver lean is driven by the driver group's rotation.z. Wheel spin comes from pivot group rotation. Real shadows are sufficient, so no extra shadow blob is needed. The camera near plane is 0.1 and far plane is 500 with a 68-degree field of view; split-screen uses 72 degrees. The follow camera targets a point four meters ahead of the kart along its heading. A bumper camera mode will sit closer and lower. Each player stores their chosen camera mode in the shell.\n\nPause will freeze the race simulation while still rendering and muting audio. The results screen will appear once when the phase becomes finished. Cup flow will record each result, advance to the next course on continue with a fresh race and same character, retry the current course on retry, and show the podium when the cup ends. A formatTime helper is being added to display elapsed time as minutes and seconds.\n\nThe time formatter will pad minutes, seconds, and milliseconds to two, two, and three digits. Lap times will be recorded incrementally as each kart crosses the start line, then shown on the results screen for a polished vertical slice feel.\n\nThe plan checklist will be updated only after the bundled HTML shell is actually created. A deduplication filter is being added to remove stadium points that are too close together, preventing zero-length track segments.\n\nbuildCourse will compute segment lengths, total length, and bounds. Linear sampling over roughly eighty segments is sufficient, so binary search is unnecessary. Course constants like roadHalf will be added. A dirt shortcut visual will appear as a chord across one offroad corner; gameplay detects this by lateral position.\n\nNo special dirt polygon is needed for the shortcut logic. The shoulder ribbon already marks the dirt area visually, and lateral distance from the centerline determines whether a cut is on road or offroad.\n\nWalls are placed on both sides of the centerline at ±wallHalf. A straight chord across the infield shortcut would cross the inner wall, so it is blocked by the corridor walls rather than being offroad.\n\nThe offroad model is being refined so the inside of the track is open grass rather than a wall, while the outside remains blocked by walls. The kart will be on asphalt only when its lateral distance from the centerline stays within roadHalf. Beyond that limit toward the outside, it enters offroad until reaching wallHalf, then hits the wall. Inside the track, offroad exists but without a wall, making shortcuts slower instead of impossible. The counterclockwise stadium path direction is being mapped to determine which side is the infield.\n\nThe track path is confirmed counterclockwise, so the infield lies to the left of travel. Negative right-lateral distance marks the inside of the track. The wall side constant is set to 'right', meaning positive lateral distance from the centerline triggers offroad before colliding with the wall.\n\nAn unbounded infield would let karts cut across the entire track and break lap progress. A far boundary is needed to keep the shortcut short grass rather than a full shortcut.\n\nProgress projection is the real danger. A shortcut across the infield would place the kart far from the current progress window, so the nearest-point search would not jump to the opposite straight. The kart would drive in slow grass instead of cutting the corner, and the CPU would stay on the line.\n\nA few-meter corner cut is acceptable. A full cross would leave the kart far from the search window and force it to steer back.\n\nThe search window is plus or minus 35 meters, which easily covers the two-meter travel distance per frame at 40 m/s.\n\nA hairpin shortcut could still fail to advance progress if its ahead point falls outside the window.\n\nThe 35-meter ahead search window makes hairpin shortcuts unreliable. Progress stalls when the nearest centerline point sits outside the window, so a shortcut cannot reliably advance progress. The safer model is a corridor with walls on both sides, keeping karts inside the ribbon. Offroad becomes shoulder strips between roadHalf and wallHalf on each side; deep cuts hit dirt then the wall.\n\nThe corridor model is finalized. A kart placed just outside roadHalf but inside wallHalf will hit the wall and slow down, confirming the offroad logic without projection bugs. The kart will be marked offroad whenever its lateral distance exceeds roadHalf. Shoulder strips will use a distinct color so offroad is visually obvious.\n\nThe design is finalized and file writing will begin. Each player will be assigned to a gamepad by index, with axes and button states read from navigator.getGamepads.\n\nKeyboard input will be tracked via a keydown/keyup set. During races, pointer-locked mouse movement and button presses map to kart controls; clicking the canvas requests pointer lock. On menus, left click confirms selections and right click goes back, while mouse movement does not steer the menu. Touch input on the settings button will open the settings panel without triggering attract-mode cancellation.\n\nA device capability check will detect coarse pointers and hover support. Touch devices without hover will see a banner requiring a controller or keyboard, and no touch joysticks will be created. A Bluetooth keyboard can still be used to bypass the gate.\n\nThe settings button will appear on both the touch gate and the main menu. Item effect tests are being stabilized by placing karts on the circuit centerline instead of open arena space, preventing them from spawning off the track and getting wall-pushed immediately.\n\nThe ricochet test will use two human-controlled karts to keep the victim stationary. Kart 1 starts eight meters ahead of kart 0, and the ricochet projectile spawns one point eight meters ahead at speed forty-six. The simulation will substep the physics so the bullet closes the gap in roughly one frame. Both karts will have neutral inputs during the test to prevent AI movement.\n\nCrownseeker and homing tests will use the same two-kart setup, with the victim placed ahead so they hold rank one. Wall bouncing is ruled out because the projectile stays on the centerline heading. The slick test places the victim behind the user at progress thirty-eight point five, about one point five meters back from the user’s position.\n\nThe slick test will place the victim kart exactly on the hazard position, set its speed to zero, and step the simulation for 0.4 seconds to verify the spin effect. Because the kart is stationary with no steering input, projectProgress will not displace it off the hazard even if its heading does not align perfectly with the centerline.\n\nAegis overlap will be tested by placing two karts at the same position and using the item. Collision resolution happens after all inputs are handled, so the victim spins in the same step.\n\nFor brownout, both karts start at equal speed. The victim receives the debuff immediately when the item is used, but speed integration runs after effects are processed. Accelerating for one second afterward should make the victim slower than the user.\n\nPilfer will swap the victim’s held item into the user’s hand in the same step. Spark will be tested by starting from zero speed and verifying the kart reaches top speed plus six. The race must begin before any item is used, so the countdown phase is skipped.\n\nA tapUse helper will press and release the use button each frame to test triSpark charging. sparkStorm will be verified by using it, confirming stormT is active, then stepping for one second and checking that speed rises from zero. scatter will assert exactly three slick hazards are created. phantom will be tested as a hazard type to see if it spins the victim longer than slick.\n\nPhantom spin will be set to 1.35 seconds and slick to 0.75 seconds so the two effects are clearly distinct. The test will assert that phantom leaves spinT at or above 1.0 while slick keeps it below 1.0. A `controlLock` field equal to spinT may also be added for clearer test assertions.\n\nPhantom will set `kart.fooled = true` so the HUD can display \"Trick crate!\", giving the test a distinct observable state beyond spin duration. Slick leaves this flag unset.\n\nCrownseeker will be tested by giving the leader a spark, then hitting them with the item. The test will assert that the held item is cleared and the victim spins, while homing only spins without dropping the held item.\n\nAn optional ricochet wall-bounce test will fire at the arena edge and verify the projectile bounces or its heading flips. The user will be placed near x=30 facing +X so the projectile hits the wall at x=35.\n\nThe ricochet test will use two projectiles: one to hit the circuit and another to bounce off the arena wall. A battle elimination test will pop balloons three times with ricochet, then verify that zero balloons eliminates a kart and the remaining kart wins. Countdown will be kept for all modes including battles. A new `raceFinishedOrder` helper will return kart names by rank for testing.\n\nThe `finishOrder` helper will return kart names, ranks, and finish times sorted by rank. No extra markdown files will be created. The test script needs fixing and the code will be written in large chunks.\n\nI will scan the code for stubs, TODOs, FIXMEs, and placeholders before finalizing. Credits.js will export a feedback text function or a string constant updated at build time. A structured source-info block will be exported from credits.js and embedded into the HTML comment by the build script so both stay synchronized. Tests will not verify the HTML comment until the bundled file exists.\n\nThe audio engine will create a single noise buffer for impact sounds and generate item effects as short square-wave arpeggios. Multiple local players will each get up to four separate audio engines.\n\nCountdown audio will use a sine wave. The AudioContext will remain suspended until the first user gesture, so attract-mode stays silent unless a prior input already unlocked it; any key or gamepad press on the title screen resumes playback. A quiet menu blip will provide subtle feedback for UI navigation.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1d34b9a2-1bb1-4d09-806a-a383d7eacf34-24","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"renderAsync\\(","path":"/tmp/grok-goal-17adb0589bdc/implementer/three.webgpu.js","head_limit":10}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3336,"output_tokens":6503,"cache_read_input_tokens":98304,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"83426eb5-85b6-41b7-a8dd-20e2fb7a03ef"}
