{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6244bdbd-b81b-4806-abed-f404518daa15-25","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"54640→\\t\\tthis._handleObjectFunction = this._renderObjectDirect;\\n\\n\\t\\t// wait for all promises setup by backends awaiting compilation/linking/pipeline creation to complete\\n\\n\\t\\tawait Promise.all( compilationPromises );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Renders the scene in an async fashion.\\n54650→\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @param {Object3D} scene - The scene or 3D object to render.\\n\\t * @param {Camera} camera - The camera.\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when the render has been finished.\\n\\t */\\n\\tasync renderAsync( scene, camera ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( this._initialized === false ) await this.init();\\n\\n54660→\\t\\tthis._renderScene( scene, camera );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Can be used to synchronize CPU operations with GPU tasks. So when this method is called,\\n\\t * the CPU waits for the GPU to complete its operation (e.g. a compute task).\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when synchronization has been finished.\\n54670→\\t */\\n\\tasync waitForGPU() {\\n\\n\\t\\tawait this.backend.waitForGPU();\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Enables or disables high precision for model-view and normal-view matrices.\\n\\t * When enabled, will use CPU 64-bit precision for higher precision instead of GPU 32-bit for higher performance.\\n54680→\\t *\\n\\t * NOTE: 64-bit precision is not compatible with `InstancedMesh` and `SkinnedMesh`.\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @param {boolean} value - Whether to enable or disable high precision.\\n\\t * @type {boolean}\\n\\t */\\n\\tset highPrecision( value ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( value === true ) {\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"54640→\\t\\tthis._handleObjectFunction = this._renderObjectDirect;\\n\\n\\t\\t// wait for all promises setup by backends awaiting compilation/linking/pipeline creation to complete\\n\\n\\t\\tawait Promise.all( compilationPromises );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Renders the scene in an async fashion.\\n54650→\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @param {Object3D} scene - The scene or 3D object to render.\\n\\t * @param {Camera} camera - The camera.\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when the render has been finished.\\n\\t */\\n\\tasync renderAsync( scene, camera ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( this._initialized === false ) await this.init();\\n\\n54660→\\t\\tthis._renderScene( scene, camera );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Can be used to synchronize CPU operations with GPU tasks. So when this method is called,\\n\\t * the CPU waits for the GPU to complete its operation (e.g. a compute task).\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when synchronization has been finished.\\n54670→\\t */\\n\\tasync waitForGPU() {\\n\\n\\t\\tawait this.backend.waitForGPU();\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Enables or disables high precision for model-view and normal-view matrices.\\n\\t * When enabled, will use CPU 64-bit precision for higher precision instead of GPU 32-bit for higher performance.\\n54680→\\t *\\n\\t * NOTE: 64-bit precision is not compatible with `InstancedMesh` and `SkinnedMesh`.\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @param {boolean} value - Whether to enable or disable high precision.\\n\\t * @type {boolean}\\n\\t */\\n\\tset highPrecision( value ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( value === true ) {\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/tmp/grok-goal-17adb0589bdc/implementer/three.webgpu.js\",\"offset\":54640,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"\\t\\tthis._handleObjectFunction = this._renderObjectDirect;\\n\\n\\t\\t// wait for all promises setup by backends awaiting compilation/linking/pipeline creation to complete\\n\\n\\t\\tawait Promise.all( compilationPromises );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Renders the scene in an async fashion.\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @param {Object3D} scene - The scene or 3D object to render.\\n\\t * @param {Camera} camera - The camera.\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when the render has been finished.\\n\\t */\\n\\tasync renderAsync( scene, camera ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( this._initialized === false ) await this.init();\\n\\n\\t\\tthis._renderScene( scene, camera );\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Can be used to synchronize CPU operations with GPU tasks. So when this method is called,\\n\\t * the CPU waits for the GPU to complete its operation (e.g. a compute task).\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @async\\n\\t * @return {Promise} A Promise that resolves when synchronization has been finished.\\n\\t */\\n\\tasync waitForGPU() {\\n\\n\\t\\tawait this.backend.waitForGPU();\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n\\t/**\\n\\t * Enables or disables high precision for model-view and normal-view matrices.\\n\\t * When enabled, will use CPU 64-bit precision for higher precision instead of GPU 32-bit for higher performance.\\n\\t *\\n\\t * NOTE: 64-bit precision is not compatible with `InstancedMesh` and `SkinnedMesh`.\\n\\t *\\n\\t * @param {boolean} value - Whether to enable or disable high precision.\\n\\t * @type {boolean}\\n\\t */\\n\\tset highPrecision( value ) {\\n\\n\\t\\tif ( value === true ) {\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":77115}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"8a370612-b51e-4ef2-b4f2-5019547b1e94"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_19","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Using await renderer.renderAsync(scene, camera) in the loop for safety.\n\nMultiple renderAsync calls should work for split screen. The HTML title and heading will be \"Kartesian Derby\". esbuild will bundle the main scripts while keeping Three.js, Rapier, and audio.js as external imports.\n\nThe HTML import map resolves external dependencies for the module script. Credits and titles will be pulled from a shared source file for both the HTML comment and the bundled about screen. I'll sanitize that text to avoid `</script>` or `-->` sequences that could break the markup. A noscript fallback message will also be added.\n\nDistinct kart shapes are being defined for each driver using varying body dimensions. Colors will be passed as numeric values to MeshToonNodeMaterial. Small dark boxes will serve as eyes on the driver faces.\n\nHair styles are being defined for each driver: pigtails for Pip, stacked spheres for Quill, a green bush for Bramble, and a flat cap for Holt. The Race.js module will be structured with imports, constants, kart and box builders, race creation, step logic, and helpers for handling, drift, items, projectiles, collisions, rankings, and countdown.\n\nRace.js will export SHIELD_KINDS, lapForProgress, and finishOrder for tests. Input.js will expose axis inversion, pad, keyboard, and mouse action builders, plus touch constants and deadzone settings. Keyboard controls are being mapped to WASD, Space, F, C, and Escape.\n\nKeyboard controls now include arrow keys, IJKL, and numpad variants. The menu will accept both arrow and WASD navigation, with Enter to confirm and Backspace or Escape to return. Driving is disabled while the menu is active.\n\nThe keyboard input logic is being simplified into a pure `actionsFromKeys` function that takes pressed key codes and returns driving or menu actions based on the active screen state.\n\nThe keyboard input function now returns confirm, back, and menu direction actions. Space is mapped to both hop and confirm, but the shell resolves this by choosing the appropriate action based on the active screen. Enter will not trigger pause for player two.\n\nP1 pause is Escape. P2 pause is Enter, which also confirms in menus. Gamepad start serves as pause. Driving keys will not trigger menu navigation. Mouse input is being mapped to steering, acceleration, braking, hop, item use, and confirm.\n\nMouse input now includes back navigation and zeroed menu axes. The shell consumes mouse delta per frame and ignores pointer lock during races. Inversion applies only to gamepad axes. Deadzone is 0.18. Menu navigation prefers the D-pad over the analog stick.\n\nHorizontal inversion will flip both steer and menuX together, since both map to the same axis. Vertical inversion only affects vertical axes like menuY. A test plan is being drafted to verify this behavior.\n\nA guard will default missing pad buttons to zero. Digital buttons require a 0.4 threshold, while triggers use 0.12. The PS5 button layout is mapped: cross, circle, square, triangle, L1, R1, L2, R2, share, options, D-pad, and four analog axes.\n\nThe input model will rely on digital gamepad buttons rather than analog axes for triggers to avoid double-counting. A combined `drivingActions` helper is being added to input.js that merges keyboard, pad, and mouse inputs into a single driving action object.\n\nThe `drivingActions` helper now merges keyboard, pad, and mouse inputs. Gamepad steering overrides keyboard steering only when its absolute value is larger. Acceleration, braking, hop, item use, camera, and pause are OR'd across all three input sources.\n\nThe `drivingActions` helper is the main entry point for the game loop. Tests will use it directly. Menu navigation axes and confirm or back buttons now merge from pad input when available. Inversion is still tested separately via `actionsFromPad` and `applyAxisInvert`.\n\nAn `edges` helper is being added to main.js. It detects single-frame transitions for confirm, back, pause, camera, menu axes, hop, and useItem by comparing the current action state against the previous one.\n\nThe simulation receives held driving states rather than edge events. Acceleration, braking, steering, and hop are held inputs, while the shell handles camera and pause edges separately. An `anyInput` helper is being added to detect user activity across keys, mouse, and gamepads for the attract screen.\n\nThe `anyInput` helper will detect gamepad stick movement above a 0.3 deadzone. A one-frame swallow flag will prevent the input that exits the attract screen from immediately triggering menu confirm. The npm package type needs fixing, and a physics deprecation warning in tests must be addressed.\n\nTests will use node:test assert. A dedicated test will verify all twelve item classes are covered and each effect function path is exercised. Physics initializes once per suite. Race simulations are pure and lightweight, so eight karts for a few seconds of offroad play adds negligible overhead.\n\nRocket start tests will use explicit messages so any failure points to the exact branch. A substepping bug was avoided where held acceleration latches too early on the first substep. The boost window logic checks timeToGo against 0.35 seconds, and test steps must account for input being computed before each step based on that remaining time.\n\nThe test loop now checks `timeToGo` before each 0.02-second step to decide whether to press acceleration. In the boost mode, acceleration is only active during the 0.35-second window, so the test presses once at 0.36 seconds and again at 0.34 seconds. In the early mode, acceleration stays true for the entire countdown, but the first press latches the early start state and subsequent presses are ignored.\n\nA speed comparison test will step the race for 0.6 seconds with acceleration after the GO signal. The early start penalty lasts 1.15 seconds, so it is still active during that window. Simulated speeds are 32.75 for boost, 13.2 for none, and 4.62 for early. Boost clearly outpaces both other modes.\n\nThe test numbers will be set to match the simulated speeds. Tests will assert only inequalities, such as boost greater than none and early less than none, rather than exact values. The drift test will use Pip with a speed of 18 to satisfy the 8-unit threshold, holding steer and hop for 1.3 seconds before releasing one frame early. Speed at the end of the charge will be recorded and compared against the release threshold.\n\nMini-turbo will add a fixed speed bonus on release that scales with drift stage. The bonus is always positive and increases at higher stages, so the kart jumps above its previous speed. Tests will verify the kart reaches stage two before releasing and that the bonus remains unclamped.\n\nThe drift release step adds the mini-turbo bonus before integrating speed toward its target. Because the bonus is larger than the tiny pull from integration in a single substep, the kart remains faster after release than before.\n\nThe drift charge loop advances one frame at a time while holding hop. The first frame latches the drift state and subsequent frames keep it active without re-triggering. After 1.2 seconds the charge threshold is reached and stage two activates. Kart speed is set to 16 with aligned heading before the hop loop begins. Full steering during drift turns hard to avoid wall collisions that could reduce speed.\n\nThe drift test risks spinning the kart into a wall. A 1.2-second full-steer charge on a stadium straight adds roughly 160 degrees of heading change, which can overshoot the track boundary.\n\nThe drift test risks a one-frame wall collision on release. The mini-turbo bonus adds speed before the wall resolves, so the kart still ends faster than before even if it hits the wall. A severe crash is unlikely in that single frame.\n\nThe drift charge risks a wall collision. A 1.2-second full-steer turn on a 120-meter straight produces roughly 82 degrees of heading change and 17 meters of turning radius. The kart could still overshoot the 8-meter track boundary.\n\nA 0.9 rad/s drift yaw rate on a straight track keeps the kart inside the 24-meter width, though the lateral offset nears the wall boundary. Repeatedly snapping the kart back to the centerline each step was rejected as cheating.\n\nDrift release bonuses will be deferred until after wall resolution. The kart stores the pending stage in `pendingTurbo` during the drift update, then applies the speed bonus and mini-turbo timer only after walls finish processing. This prevents a one-frame wall collision from wiping out the boost before it can take effect.\n\nThe drift test will record the highest `driftStage` reached during the charge loop and assert it equals 2. It will also verify that `miniTurboT` is greater than zero immediately after release.\n\nThe pending turbo bonus will be applied immediately on release but deferred until after wall resolution within the same update sequence. Scene disposal and memory cleanup are being skipped for the demo. The grandstand and trees total roughly 120 meshes, which is acceptable. A single gradient texture will be created once. Items will use bright toon materials to stay visible, with a red-orange sphere for the ricochet item.\n\nItem meshes are now defined. Homing is crimson, crownseeker dark with gold, slick is a flattened yellow sphere, and phantom is a purple-tinted yellow cube. Sparks will be boost flame particles rather than world objects. The flame cone behind the kart will be drawn in animate. The countdown will render as a large DOM overlay instead of 3D text. After GO appears, it stays visible for 0.8 seconds before fading.\n\nUI strings will live in the main file rather than a separate copy module. Trial mode starts with triSpark and no item boxes. Versus mode fills to eight entrants using CPUs, while battle and trial do not. Cup mode uses one human player and seven CPUs. A `playerPlace` helper is being added to find the human kart by its index.\n\nThe `playerPlace` helper now returns the kart's rank or the total kart count if no player kart is found. Cup races award points from a fixed table: 10, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. A `recordCupResult` function tallies these points and tracks each race's course and place. Entrants finishing after fourth place trigger a retry action with zero gain.\n\n`recordCupResult` advances the cup index and returns a finished action once all courses are completed. A `noteTrial` helper stores the best time per course and flags whether the current run improved on the previous record.\n\n`noteTrial` now returns `improved: prev == null || time < prev`, so a tied time does not count as an improvement. Session helpers and attract idle constants will live in `modes.js`. An `isAttractIdleScreen` helper is being added to identify the title, help, settings, and about screens.\n\nThe attract idle timer will only count title, help, settings, and about screens. Character select, player selection, and course selection are excluded from the idle window so the attract mode does not interrupt active setup.\n\nThe attract idle timer now excludes pause and results screens. Any input during attract mode returns the player to the title screen rather than the previous menu. Session input resets the idle counter on non-race screens. No README will be added.\n\nThe bundled HTML filename and folder name are finalized. Files will be written in batches across math, titles, characters, courses, items, input, modes, race, physics, scene, audio, main, tests, and build. A credits draft is being prepared and test counts will be updated later. Honest engine feedback is being considered.\n\nThe WebGPU renderer in three r180 with MeshToonNodeMaterial and gradient maps provides real quantized lighting without a custom shader pipeline. Rapier 0.19.3 inlines WASM so the physics world boots from a CDN module. MeshToonNodeMaterial is confirmed on the webgpu entry. ConeGeometry and Fog are missing from that build, so props will use boxes, spheres, and cylinders instead. Rapier initialization prints a deprecation warning from inside the package.\n\nThe kart ruleset was built from scratch because neither Three.js nor Rapier ships a racing game. The hardest part was keeping one deterministic race step that tests can drive while the rendered world still steps a real Rapier physics world every frame, and handling split-screen viewports on WebGPURenderer. The deprecation warning on `RAPIER.init()` and the missing primitive geometries in the webgpu bundle are the remaining items to remove or overhaul.\n\nThe geometry set will include classic shapes and Fog on the `three/webgpu` entry so cel-shaded props do not need to be faked.\n\nPrompt feedback: The feature list is concrete and gives a clear finish line. The filename template uses spaces while the plan hyphenates them. \"Engine feedback\" could be ambiguous between the agent harness and the game engine. A headless machine running WebGPU without a fallback is a concern.\n\nA real 4-player DualSense test across cup, trial, and battle modes is impossible here because visual review is forbidden and the environment may lack a WebGPU adapter or physical controller. I will refine the credits to list only the HTML comment rather than all 50 titles.\n\nThe chosen title will appear on the about screen as the product name. The HTML comment will contain all fifty candidates and the RNG result. Source information, engine feedback, and prompt feedback will also be placed in that comment.\n\nA `<template id=\"about-text\">` element will feed the about screen from a single `credits.js` export. The build script will inject that same text into the HTML comment. The rendered text preserves whitespace and avoids `-->` sequences. Exact headings like \"Source Information\" and \"Testing Feedback\" will appear in the about screen.\n\nThe about screen will list the allowed testing parameters, usage unknown, model and reasoning effort, and a tech line naming Three.js r180 WebGPURenderer, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio API, and Gamepad API with no WebGL fallback. The art line will describe the stylized cel-shaded look using MeshToonNodeMaterial with a four-stop nearest-neighbor gradient map and inverse-hull outlines.\n\nAnimation will use procedural per-frame transforms for wheel spin, driver lean, crate tumble, drift sparks, and boost flames. No skeletal clips are used.\n\nAn integration smoke test will verify that a fresh countdown eventually reaches racing and moves the kart when acceleration is held. A boxes-respawn test will also be added.\n\nThe invert Y axis will be tested through drivingActions to confirm it does not alter steering.\n\nCheat attract mode will ignore offroad speed penalties and recover faster, plus grant a start boost. CPU steering look-ahead is 12 plus speed times 0.4. Sharp turns above 0.9 radians at speeds over 16 trigger a light brake to prevent wall slams. The handling logic will reduce the acceleration target when both brake and accelerate are held.\n\nThe CPU acceleration target is capped at 45% of top speed when both brake and accelerate are held. Reverse gear only activates for humans if brake is pressed while acceleration is released and speed is three or below; otherwise the kart decelerates hard.\n\nThe offroad speed factor and drift thresholds will remain unnamed constants; tests only check inequalities. Scene materials will be pooled by color key to limit creation, and karts, trees, and walls will all cast shadows.\n\nRoad and ground will receive shadows. The directional light target will be added to the scene so the shadow camera can aim at it. Shadow camera bounds will be set from each course's bounding box. Two warm point lights with distance and decay will be added for the lantern course.\n\nPoint light intensity will be 30 and distance 40 to suit the toon material. A sky sphere at radius 400 with a basic back-side material will wrap the scene. MeshBasicNodeMaterial supports BackSide via setValues. The first batch of files to write is math, titles, characters, courses, items, input, and modes.\n\nThe remaining files to write are race.js, physics.js, audio.js, scene.js, main.js, credits.js, tests, and build. The stadium course geometry will be computed carefully and validated with a quick node test. Zero-length segments will be filtered out. Item boxes will be placed using progress and lateral offsets sampled from the course path. Battle mode will also include item boxes.\n\nBattle mode will place item boxes at the four corners and two mid-sides of the arena, avoiding the center obstacle. Without these items, players would only pop balloons by bumping into aegis-kart drivers, which is too slow.\n\nItem boxes will be enabled in battle mode but disabled in trial mode. The `createRace` function will place arena boxes at fixed spots and track boxes along the path. Collection radius is 2.4 units, ignoring vertical distance on circuits. Starting inventory is empty except in trial mode. Battle players can hold items from boxes, and roulette weights will rank by balloon count then distance rather than race progress.\n\nBattle roulette will rank karts by balloons remaining, then hits dealt. The player with the most balloons is treated as the leader and receives weaker items. This creates a catch-up incentive instead of a simple random distribution.\n\nEliminated karts will be parked off the track as non-colliding ghosts with zero speed and a dimmed visual. Collision resolution will skip them entirely. The last kart standing wins; if every kart is eliminated, the result is a draw.\n\nInflicting damage on an eliminated kart will be a no-op. The fixed-step loop caps event array length at 32 and trims excess entries to 20. It branches to countdown logic during the countdown phase, skips all other phases, and otherwise advances the race clock.\n\nThe fixed-step loop iterates over karts, skipping finished and eliminated ones. It resolves walls, kart collisions, cones, pending turbos, projectiles, hazards, boxes, rank updates, and end-of-race checks in sequence.\n\nDrift release defers turbo activation to the wall-resolution phase so the test sees the speed increase after movement. Hopping and drift charging happen inside stepKart before movement.\n\nThe `updateDrift` function now checks for hop input and release. A valid hop triggers drift charge and a 0.32-second hop window, but only when speed exceeds 7 and steering is strong enough. Otherwise it falls back to a basic hop with the same timing.\n\nDrift charging continues while the hop input is held and steering aligns with the drift direction. The charge stage advances through three tiers at 0.55 and 1.15 seconds, each adding extra yaw to the kart heading. Releasing the input triggers a pending turbo based on the current stage and resets the drift state.\n\nSteering heading is now calculated with grip, reverse direction, and brownout damping. During spin, steering input is ignored while the kart spins and speed decays.\n\nDuring spin, acceleration input is suppressed so the kart cannot instantly accelerate out of the spin. The desired speed function applies a 1.22x cheat multiplier and a 0.56x offroad penalty, both ignored in cheat mode. Brownout further reduces top speed to 0.55x.\n\nBoost and mini-turbo stages add 7 plus 2 per stage to the top speed cap. The start penalty multiplier is 0.4, but only while no boost is active. During spin, desired speed is zero. Braking at or below 3 speed returns reverse gear; above 3 it returns 42 percent of top speed when acceleration is held, otherwise zero. Acceleration rate uses the kart's full acceleration value when targeting higher speed, otherwise the brake value or a default of 6.\n\nThe acceleration rate is reduced to 35 percent while a start penalty is active, and it is clamped to at least 30 during boost or mini-turbo. A `moveToward` helper smoothly interpolates speed toward the target within the allowed delta. Position integration uses sine and cosine of heading multiplied by speed and delta time. For circuit courses, vertical height is sampled from the terrain plus any hop offset; for arena mode, only the hop offset is applied.\n\nA `hopHeight` helper computes vertical lift as a sine curve peaking at 1.15 units over the 0.32-second hop window. After integrating position, circuit karts are projected onto the course path to derive lateral offset and along-track distance.\n\nThe along-track distance is omitted from the kart state because the course projection already yields the nearest sample point. Offroad status is derived from lateral offset exceeding half the road width. Lap display is computed from progress and total course length. A kart finishes after three full laps, with its finish time assigned from the race clock.\n\nThe `project` function searches a 50-slice window around the kart's current progress to find the nearest course sample. It then refines that guess by testing ±1 meter in 0.25-unit steps to pin down the exact along-track position.\n\nThe `project` function now returns the exact course sample after refining the guess. Wall resolution will reuse the lateral offset computed during `follow`, which runs inside `stepKart` before walls are processed.\n\nThe step sequence is finalized: move and follow progress, resolve kart collisions, then resolve walls. After wall pushes shift positions, the kart is re-projected onto the course to update lateral offset and along-track progress. The offroad flag is recalculated after clamping so it stays accurate. CPU stuck recovery from slowT will also be applied inside stepKart.\n\nItem use and roulette logic run at the end of stepKart before kart collisions. Projectiles fired during that same frame move immediately in stepProjectiles. Aegis status is active before kart collision resolution.\n\nBoxes are updated globally per frame. Each box timer decrements and respawns after its cooldown. Collection checks run after movement by comparing every kart to every box.\n\nA kart can only collect a box if it is finished, eliminated, holding an item, or already in roulette. The pickup radius is 2.3 units. Collecting a box sets the kart's roulette timer to 1.7 seconds and records its current rank as the place value. The race event logs the pickup. Roulette resolution happens inside stepKart once the timer expires.\n\nCPU karts stop roulette early at 0.35 seconds, while attract mode triggers at 0.25 seconds so items appear quickly. Pressing the use-item button during roulette halts it immediately; the kart skips item activation for that same frame and can use it on the next.\n\nThe early return during roulette is removed so movement still occurs; only the held-item activation is skipped for that frame. Cheat mode now grants a random item to any kart that has none, is not in roulette, and has no lock, with a brief lock afterward. CPU item usage logic is being simplified so attract-mode karts actually fire items.\n\nCPU input now activates the use-item button whenever a kart holds an item and has no lock or roulette active. Shield items are sometimes paired with a brake input to trail behind other karts. The CPU release latch is cleared each frame so the button can be pressed again.\n\nThe CPU release latch is cleared each frame so the use-item button can be pressed again. For multi-charge items like triSpark, the CPU must explicitly release the input between presses to avoid firing all charges at once. A `_itemTap` toggle was considered but rejected because it would flicker every frame.\n\nThe CPU item activation logic is finalized: it presses useItem only when no lock is active, and the held-item function sets a 0.6-second lock that suppresses further presses until it expires. The itemLock timer decrements every frame inside stepKart. A GO label timer will be added to the countdown transition, and the countdown phase will not move any karts.\n\nWhen the countdown ends, the race phase switches to racing and a GO label appears. CPUs and cheat karts receive an immediate start boost that raises their speed toward top speed and extends the boost timer. Karts with early starts are penalized by zeroing speed and applying a 1.2-second delay.\n\nThe CPU start boost is being reduced to a small advantage. A human who waits at the start window will no longer be left far behind by a large speed gap.\n\nCPU and cheat karts now spawn at 35 percent top speed with no extra boost timer. Human karts who nail the start window reach 90 percent top speed plus their boost. The test harness only uses human karts, so the CPU branch is irrelevant there.\n\nThe countdown label updates from 3 down to GO as the timer nears zero. A race event is logged for each change except GO. The initial beep is emitted in createRace because the first label stays at 3 until the timer reaches 2.\n\nThe GO label timer will be tracked in race state and decremented during racing. A `formatTime` helper is being added to modes.js. Menu navigation will wrap around for player count, course selection, and character slots. Player count options are 2, 3, and 4. Battle mode skips course selection entirely. Trial and versus modes let each player pick a course. Cup mode follows the preset cup order. Character selection uses left and right to change the character for the focused slot, up and down to switch slots, and confirm to start.\n\nDefault characters are Pip for P1, Quill for P2, Bramble for P3, and Holt for P4. Stats will appear as one-line descriptive blurb text instead of dense number tables. The results screen will display places in large type. A visible keyboard focus indicator will be added to the selected menu row.\n\nStyles will live inside the HTML build template rather than a separate file. The UI design uses a transparent background over the canvas with dark translucent rounded panels and large type. An amber bar will indicate the selected menu item. The font stack is Trebuchet MS, Avenir Next, Segoe UI, and sans-serif. The title wordmark will be huge. HUD safe margins are set to 4vh. Split borders will be drawn as inset colored box-shadows on absolutely positioned HUD clusters.\n\nThe product title h1 will remain in the DOM but be hidden during races via CSS. The \"Grand Cup\" button serves as the start control, and the first menu button gets an id of `start-game`. A `build.mjs` script will embed the CSS and bundled JavaScript into the final HTML file.\n\nThe static HTML shell will contain only the canvas, a UI wrapper, a HUD container, and two hidden gates for touch and settings. JavaScript will populate the panel dynamically.\n\nThe UI will mount synchronously before any GPU or module awaits. This ensures the test harness sees the buttons immediately on load, while the GPU boot proceeds asynchronously without blocking the DOM.\n\nThe boot function is invoked without a top-level await so module evaluation completes and the DOM mounts before the load event fires. GPU initialization runs asynchronously afterward, which risks the test capturing the canvas before its size is set.\n\nThe canvas size will be set synchronously on the first frame before any GPU initialization. A `data-ready` attribute will signal when boot completes so the launch script can wait for it. This prevents the test harness from capturing the canvas before its layout dimensions are finalized.\n\nA `fitCanvas` helper will read the canvas's client width and height immediately during mount, before GPU initialization, to prevent zero or default dimensions if layout is not yet computed. The renderer will call `setSize` again after GPU setup completes.\n\nThe canvas sizing helper will guard against zero or negative dimensions before applying them. It will run at boot, after GPU initialization, and on window resize.\n\nA `data-boot` attribute will signal readiness to the test harness. If WebGPU initialization fails, the gate will display a message requiring WebGPU and a local server while keeping the title visible. An aria label will also be added.\n\nThe minimap drawing function will live in main.js so courses.js stays pure and avoids DOM dependencies. A quick sample-length test will run after writing to verify the bundle. race.js is being written with extra care.\n\nI am checking the code for accidental TODO, FIXME, placeholder, and stub markers. The name startBoost is acceptable. I will avoid HTML placeholder attributes. No plan deviations are needed.\n\nAll item classes are mapped to their in-game names. Shield-style items—ricochet, homing, and slick—will leave a trailing state when the kart holds them and brakes while pressing useItem.\n\nThe held-item logic now handles trailing states. When a kart holds a shield-style item and brakes while pressing useItem, it enters a trailing state. Another kart hitting that trail point causes a spin and clears the trail. During trailing, pressing useItem without braking throws the held item; pressing useItem while braking keeps the trail active.\n\nA small mesh per kart will render the held trailing item behind it in the scene. The code will be written in three waves: foundation modules first, then race, physics, scene, audio, and main, followed by tests and build. The diorama shown when not racing is also being considered.\n\nThe menu diorama will show the selected course with karts parked on the grid. A frozen race state and a time-based animation loop will keep the scene alive even when no race is active. Drivers bob and crates spin continuously, while kart wheels idle slowly. This satisfies the requirement that the render loop animates karts even during menu navigation.\n\nThe menu camera will orbit the course at a fixed radius. It will switch to kart-following mode when a race begins, and rebuild the scene whenever the selected course changes. A subtle fog-like darkening effect will be applied using the sky color. I am now starting to write the main, scene, and race modules carefully to avoid syntax errors.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The random pick is **Kartesian Derby**. Next I’ll put the race rules in testable modules, then wrap them in the WebGPU game."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d5cfdad9-ee49-4440-b999-09ae86682d24-26","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/package.json","content":"{\n  \"name\": \"kartesian-derby\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"node --test test/game.test.mjs\",\n    \"build\": \"node build.mjs\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@dimforge/rapier3d-compat\": \"0.19.3\",\n    \"esbuild\": \"0.25.0\"\n  }\n}\n"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d5cfdad9-ee49-4440-b999-09ae86682d24-27","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/src/math.js","content":"export function clamp(value, min, max) {\n  return Math.max(min, Math.min(max, value));\n}\n\nexport function wrapAngle(angle) {\n  const tau = Math.PI * 2;\n  let a = (angle + Math.PI) % tau;\n  if (a < 0) a += tau;\n  return a - Math.PI;\n}\n\nexport function moveToward(current, target, maxDelta) {\n  if (current < target) return Math.min(target, current + maxDelta);\n  if (current > target) return Math.max(target, current - maxDelta);\n  return current;\n}\n\nexport function lerp(a, b, t) {\n  return a + (b - a) * t;\n}\n\nexport function mulberry32(seed) {\n  let a = seed >>> 0;\n  return function rng() {\n    a |= 0;\n    a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0;\n    let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), 1 | a);\n    t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t;\n    return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;\n  };\n}\n\nexport function formatTime(seconds) {\n  if (seconds == null || !Number.isFinite(seconds)) return \"--:--.---\";\n  const sign = seconds < 0 ? \"-\" : \"\";\n  const abs = Math.abs(seconds);\n  const minutes = Math.floor(abs / 60);\n  const whole = Math.floor(abs - minutes * 60);\n  const ms = Math.floor((abs - Math.floor(abs)) * 1000);\n  return `${sign}${minutes}:${String(whole).padStart(2, \"0\")}.${String(ms).padStart(3, \"0\")}`;\n}\n\nexport function ordinal(n) {\n  const mod10 = n % 10;\n  const mod100 = n % 100;\n  if (mod10 === 1 && mod100 !== 11) return `${n}st`;\n  if (mod10 === 2 && mod100 !== 12) return `${n}nd`;\n  if (mod10 === 3 && mod100 !== 13) return `${n}rd`;\n  return `${n}th`;\n}\n"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d5cfdad9-ee49-4440-b999-09ae86682d24-28","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/src/titles.js","content":"// 50 original riffs. Index 21 was chosen by node:crypto.randomInt(0, 50).\nexport const TITLE_CANDIDATES = [\n  \"Marmalade Kart 64\",\n  \"Marquee Kart Rally\",\n  \"Narrow Kart Grand\",\n  \"Kart Blanche Prix\",\n  \"Kartastrophe Cup\",\n  \"Kartwheel Classic\",\n  \"Shopping Kart 64\",\n  \"Upstart Kart 64\",\n  \"Marble Kart Derby\",\n  \"Marigold Prix 64\",\n  \"Marshmallow Kart\",\n  \"Mariner Kart Cup\",\n  \"Marzipan Grand Prix\",\n  \"Depart Kart Derby\",\n  \"Rampart Kart Rally\",\n  \"Smartkart Sixtyfour\",\n  \"Heartkart Grand\",\n  \"Artkart Invitational\",\n  \"Startkart Classic\",\n  \"Quartkart Cup\",\n  \"Polar Kart 64\",\n  \"Kartesian Derby\",\n  \"Go Kart Gogh\",\n  \"Lowpoly Kart 64\",\n  \"Polygon Kart Prix\",\n  \"Rumble Kart Rally\",\n  \"Cartridge Kart Cup\",\n  \"Analog Kart 64\",\n  \"Driftkart Deluxe\",\n  \"Peelout Kart Prix\",\n  \"Miniturbo Kart\",\n  \"Shellshock Karting\",\n  \"Coinspill Kart Cup\",\n  \"Balloonkart Battle\",\n  \"Rocketstart Kart\",\n  \"Toonkart Turnpike\",\n  \"Neon Kart 64\",\n  \"Gumball Kart Grand\",\n  \"Soapbox Kart 64\",\n  \"Windup Kart Way\",\n  \"Pinwheel Kart Prix\",\n  \"Lantern Kart League\",\n  \"Corkscrew Kart Cup\",\n  \"Sundae Kart Speedway\",\n  \"Sparkplug Kart\",\n  \"Haybale Kart Derby\",\n  \"Whisker Wheel Prix\",\n  \"Jellybean Grand Prix\",\n  \"Puddlejump Derby\",\n  \"Tumbleturn Trophy\",\n];\n\nexport const TITLE_INDEX = 21;\nexport const GAME_TITLE = TITLE_CANDIDATES[TITLE_INDEX];\nexport const GAME_SLUG = \"Kartesian-Derby\";\nexport const RNG_NOTE = \"node:crypto.randomInt(0, 50) returned 21\";\n"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d5cfdad9-ee49-4440-b999-09ae86682d24-29","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/src/characters.js","content":"export const CHARACTERS = [\n  {\n    id: \"pip\",\n    name: \"Pip\",\n    klass: \"feather\",\n    blurb: \"Tiny, twitchy, and first into the corner.\",\n    accel: 22,\n    brake: 36,\n    top: 23,\n    reverse: 8,\n    handle: 2.75,\n    weight: 0.72,\n    color: 0xff4d6d,\n    accent: 0xffe08a,\n    cloth: 0xfff4ea,\n    body: [0.72, 0.28, 1.12],\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"quill\",\n    name: \"Quill\",\n    klass: \"light\",\n    blurb: \"Quick off the line and fond of a long hop.\",\n    accel: 18,\n    brake: 32,\n    top: 25.5,\n    reverse: 7.6,\n    handle: 2.4,\n    weight: 0.86,\n    color: 0xffd23f,\n    accent: 0xfff6d0,\n    cloth: 0xf7f1ff,\n    body: [0.82, 0.32, 1.28],\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"bramble\",\n    name: \"Bramble\",\n    klass: \"middle\",\n    blurb: \"A square deal in a square kart.\",\n    accel: 14.5,\n    brake: 28,\n    top: 27.5,\n    reverse: 7,\n    handle: 2.05,\n    weight: 1,\n    color: 0x2fbf71,\n    accent: 0xd8ffe4,\n    cloth: 0xf3fff6,\n    body: [0.96, 0.38, 1.46],\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"holt\",\n    name: \"Holt\",\n    klass: \"heavy\",\n    blurb: \"Slow to wake. Awful to bump.\",\n    accel: 10.5,\n    brake: 24,\n    top: 30.5,\n    reverse: 6.4,\n    handle: 1.65,\n    weight: 1.48,\n    color: 0x3d5afe,\n    accent: 0xd6dcff,\n    cloth: 0xeef1ff,\n    body: [1.16, 0.46, 1.68],\n  },\n];\n\nexport function characterById(id) {\n  return CHARACTERS.find((c) => c.id === id) || CHARACTERS[0];\n}\n\nexport function fieldEntrants(humanIds, mode) {\n  const humans = humanIds.map((id, index) => {\n    const character = characterById(id);\n    return {\n      characterId: character.id,\n      name: character.name,\n      isPlayer: true,\n      playerIndex: index,\n    };\n  });\n  if (mode === \"battle\" || mode === \"trial\") return humans;\n  const entrants = humans.slice();\n  let cursor = 0;\n  while (entrants.length < 8) {\n    const character = CHARACTERS[cursor % CHARACTERS.length];\n    cursor += 1;\n    const taken = entrants.some((e) => e.name === character.name);\n    entrants.push({\n      characterId: character.id,\n      name: taken ? `${character.name} ${cursor}` : character.name,\n      isPlayer: false,\n      playerIndex: null,\n    });\n  }\n  return entrants;\n}\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":555,"output_tokens":18284,"cache_read_input_tokens":108288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"8527ec87-aaba-4c5e-a91b-02c4826c93ca"}
