off each other and off the wall (0.414924ms)\\n✔ each item class changes racer state (8.873791ms)\\n✖ item roulette favors weaker items for the leader and boxes come back (3.194877ms)\\n✔ cup continue rule, trial best, and battle elimination (0.800288ms)\\n✔ time trial starts with a multi-charge boost and no boxes (0.195431ms)\\n✔ attract idle starts at 10 seconds and clears on input (0.172226ms)\\n✖ invert toggles negate only the requested gamepad axis (0.432594ms)\\n✔ touch-only devices are told a controller or keyboard is required (0.072358ms)\\n✔ shipped Rapier world moves a dynamic body (41.479686ms)\\nℹ tests 13\\nℹ suites 0\\nℹ pass 11\\nℹ fail 2\\nℹ cancelled 0\\nℹ skipped 0\\nℹ todo 0\\nℹ duration_ms 175.505132\\n\\n✖ failing tests:\\n\\ntest at test/game.test.mjs:328:1\\n✖ item roulette favors weaker items for the leader and boxes come back (3.194877ms)\\n  AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal:\\n  \\n  false !== true\\n  \\n      at TestContext.<anonymous> (file:///home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/test/game.test.mjs:352:10)\\n      at Test.runInAsyncScope (node:async_hooks:227:14)\\n      at Test.run (node:internal/test_runner/test:1402:25)\\n      at Test.processPendingSubtests (node:internal/test_runner/test:974:18)\\n      at Test.postRun (node:internal/test_runner/test:1542:19)\\n      at Test.run (node:internal/test_runner/test:1467:12)\\n      at process.processTicksAndRejections (node:internal/process/task_queues:104:5)\\n      at async Test.processPendingSubtests (node:internal/test_runner/test:974:7) {\\n    generatedMessage: true,\\n    code: 'ERR_ASSERTION',\\n    actual: false,\\n    expected: true,\\n    operator: 'strictEqual',\\n    diff: 'simple'\\n  }\\n\\ntest at test/game.test.mjs:432:1\\n✖ invert toggles negate only the requested gamepad axis (0.432594ms)\\n  AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal:\\n  + actual - expected\\n  \\n  + 0\\n  - -0\\n  \\n      at TestContext.<anonymous> (file:///home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/test/game.test.mjs:441:10)\\n      at Test.runInAsyncScope (node:async_hooks:227:14)\\n      at Test.run (node:internal/test_runner/test:1402:25)\\n      at Test.processPendingSubtests (node:internal/test_runner/test:974:18)\\n      at Test.postRun (node:internal/test_runner/test:1542:19)\\n      at Test.run (node:internal/test_runner/test:1467:12)\\n      at process.processTicksAndRejections (node:internal/process/task_queues:104:5)\\n      at async Test.processPendingSubtests (node:internal/test_runner/test:974:7) {\\n    generatedMessage: true,\\n    code: 'ERR_ASSERTION',\\n    actual: 0,\\n    expected: -0,\\n    operator: 'strictEqual',\\n    diff: 'simple'\\n  }\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"command\":\"cd \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637\\\" && node --test test/game.test.mjs\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Run shipped race logic unit tests\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on%2Fwork/01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862/terminal/call-1da71706-63e6-4e14-acc3-ceaf50e9095a-40.log\",\"total_bytes\":4316}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"03359b25-f8e9-4456-a289-a89bc758feda"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_25","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Two failures were identified. The first is a race condition in box respawn.\n\nThe 5.3-second test step was clamped to 3 seconds by `stepRace`, which only advances the simulation up to 3 seconds per call. The box respawn timer is 5 seconds, so it never completed before the assertion failed.\n\nThe 3-second cap should be raised or the test split into multiple steps. The real game advances time in small chunks, so the respawn timer needs to accumulate to its full duration.\n\nThe second failure is a strict equality mismatch between 0 and -0. The Y-axis value of 0.44 falls below the 0.55 threshold, so both menuY values are 0. The test asserts that flipY.menuY equals -plain.menuY, but JavaScript's Object.is treats 0 and -0 as unequal.\n\nThe Y-axis test will use 0.8 to guarantee menuY reaches ±1 and avoids the -0 mismatch. The box respawn test will step in 0.5-second chunks over six seconds, since the 3-second cap limits each call.\n\nThe Y-invert logic is sound; only the test needs adjustment. Next I will write the scene, audio, main, credits, and build files. I also plan to suppress repeated wall-impact events by tracking a `wasWalled` flag so collisions are logged only on entry.\n\nA 0.35-second cooldown will be added to wall events to prevent audio spam. The `wallLock` flag will be decremented inside `stepKart`. The attract CPU logic is acceptable as-is.\n\nI am now drafting main.js. It imports the Three.js WebGPU renderer, audio module, character and course databases, credits text, input definitions, item data, and utility functions.\n\nmain.js will also import cup and session helpers from modes.js, physics utilities from physics.js, race logic from race.js, the game title from titles.js, and scene functions from scene.js.\n\nscene.js will export a renderer factory, a view factory, an update function, layout helpers, and canvas resizing. Camera logic is still undecided between main and scene.\n\nThe WebGPU renderer will use antialiasing, SRGB color space, ACES filmic tone mapping, and PCF shadow maps. The gate check stays in main.js before scene initialization. The view object will hold the scene, cameras, karts, boxes, projectiles, hazards, cone, and lights.\n\nThe view object will not include a minimap. A track ribbon function is being built. Item meshes will be pooled as sixteen reusable spheres or cubes that are repositioned. Scene.js will be written comprehensively. Outlines will use a separate child mesh with BackSide material so they do not receive the parent's material. Wheel groups will use a pivot mesh rotated to face the ground, spinning on its x-axis. Shared wheel geometry will be reused, but box geometry will remain unique per character.\n\nTree trunks and crowns will share geometry. Materials will be cached by color. Shadow settings are being assigned: road receives shadows, walls cast and receive, karts cast shadows. The directional light shadow map resolution is 2048. A checkered start line will alternate boxes. Item boxes will be nested groups with an outer yellow cube and an inner white cube that rotates.\n\nInactive boxes will be hidden. The cone will be an orange sphere positioned from physics data. Drift sparks will be five spheres per kart, visible only during drift and offset behind the kart with a sine wave. Boost will show a scaled sphere flame behind the kart. Driver bob and lean will animate in updateView. The held item mesh will attach to the kart group, sitting behind when trailing or above when leading. Roulette will not render a world mesh; the HUD will cycle names instead. The DOM UI will live in main.js inside a mount function that arranges the panel, HUD clusters, countdown, and pause controls.\n\nThe menu renderer will use `document.createElement` and `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` to avoid XSS. It will clear the panel, add a kicker heading, and render each menu line as a button with an \"is-selected\" class for the focused item.\n\nMenu buttons will use click events for confirmation. Settings will have two toggle buttons. The about screen will render in a scrollable pre block with a back button. Character selection will display large cards. A session.draft object will hold the current mode, course, player count, characters, and slot.\n\nThe title menu now has a concrete flow. Cup mode starts with one player and the character screen. Versus and battle modes let the user pick two to four players, then route to the course screen for versus or the character screen for battle. Trial mode routes to the course screen first, then characters. Help, settings, and about return to the title. A `beginRace` function is being drafted to resolve the course from the draft state.\n\nThe `beginRace` function now selects kart IDs from the draft state, caps them for cup and trial modes, builds the field entrants, and creates the race with a random seed. It then instantiates the physics world and scene view before switching to the race screen. Rebuilding the world each time may be slow, so disposing the old world is being considered.\n\nRapier worlds will be freed with `world.free()` when present. The attract mode will begin a race, free it after completion, and start the next course. Input will exit attract mode before stepping. Pause will halt the race and show resume and quit controls. Results will appear after a normal phase finishes; attract mode will restart instead. Engine audio will drain events and scale to kart speeds. Camera cycling will wrap at edges. Split-screen will use one camera per human kart. Viewport layout is being planned.\n\nThe HUD will use four absolute-positioned clusters, each 280 pixels wide. Each cluster contains a place indicator, a lap counter, a speed display, an item slot, and a 160×160 minimap canvas. CSS classes like `players-1` and `players-2` will set the top and left offsets for each player.\n\nThe countdown will be centered and oversized. A slim eight-name order list will sit on the right for single-player races; split-screen will omit it to avoid clutter, leaving each cluster to show its own place. Place text will be the largest type, speed will display in km/h, and the item slot will show either a held item name with trailing status and charge count, or \"Roulette\" cycling through all item names.\n\nLap counters will display as \"Lap 1 / 3\", while battle mode shows balloon counts like \"Balloons 3\". Position ordinals and a \"Finished\" overlay will appear on each cluster. The next files to write are audio.js, scene.js, main.js, credits, and the build step. A touch gate element will be hidden by default and revealed via settings on load.\n\nThe touch gate will stay visible over the menu and race screens, but hidden during the race so it does not cover the HUD. It will display a message and a settings button.\n\nThe touch gate will be a fixed bottom banner centered horizontally, positioned below the title if necessary. Virtual joysticks are excluded; keyboard input remains available. The product title h1 will stay visible on the title screen and hide during races, attract mode, and pause.\n\nA `fitBuffer` helper will set the canvas size from its container or window, and it will also configure the renderer pixel ratio. The `data-boot` attribute will transition from \"booting\" to either \"ready\" or \"gate\".\n\nWebGPURenderer sizing is confirmed: setting the drawing buffer to `w * pixelRatio` while keeping the CSS width at 100% ensures the buffer matches the container. The `fit` helper will run after initialization, on resize, and before the first render. The main animation loop will clamp delta time to 0.05 seconds, update the physics state, fit the canvas if necessary, and then render the camera views.\n\nThe renderer will enable scissor testing and loop through each camera viewport, updating the aspect ratio and projection matrix before rendering asynchronously. A menu diorama view will be built at startup after GPU initialization so the title screen has a backdrop while the physics world is ready.\n\nThe boot sequence is now fully defined. It mounts the DOM, checks secure context and GPU support, requests an adapter, creates the renderer, initializes a live physics world for the menu diorama so cones fall, builds a display race with karts on the grid, constructs the view, starts the animation loop, and sets the boot state to ready. If any step fails, the gate appears and WebGL does not start. The menu camera will orbit, while the race camera will follow the kart during races, attract mode, pause, and results; results can freeze the scene behind the panel.\n\nThe last frame will keep rendering during results so the camera can remain still. A `dropWorld` helper will free the old physics world before rebuilding it for each race, with a try/catch fallback if Rapier 0.19 lacks the `free()` method.\n\nThe menu diorama will keep karts on the grid with idle wheel spins and let the cone physics world step independently. During races, only the race world steps. The engine will sync the cone's position into `race.conePos` after each step and apply any pending impulse.\n\nThe cone impulse will be applied one frame after the physics step, since `stepRace` sets it before the next world update. The countdown phase will still advance normally because `stepRace` handles it.\n\nThe menu display race will remain frozen in countdown because it is not stepped. Only the active race and attract worlds step during their respective screens. A `startAttract` function will cycle through courses using a modulo index, beginning on cinder with cheats enabled.\n\nAttract mode will use cup mode with zero human players, spawning eight CPU karts that receive start speed and cheat items. The race ends when one kart finishes, then the next course begins. Battle mode is skipped for attract since it returns no entrants. If input interrupts attract, the race is dropped and the view returns to the menu.\n\nA new `ensureSim` helper will rebuild the physics world and scene view only when the active course or race changes. It frees the old world first, then creates a fresh one and builds the scene. This avoids unnecessary recreation of the menu diorama.\n\nA `requestSim` lock will prevent overlapping async course switches. It returns early if the target is already active or a load is in progress, and the frame loop keeps the old view until the new simulation finishes.\n\nThe input handler can be async, so the race entry flow will await `createRaceWorld` before swapping the scene and setting the screen to \"race\". The menu boot sequence already waits for the initial world creation.\n\nA `disposeView` helper will traverse the scene graph and dispose of geometries and materials when swapping views to prevent memory leaks during long attract loops. Reusing geometries across rebuilds is avoided because disposal is not fully idempotent.\n\nTo prevent memory leaks during the endless attract loop, `buildScene` will return a `dispose` function that gathers all geometries into a Set and frees them in one pass. This avoids double-disposal warnings and keeps the session lightweight.\n\nThe next files to write are scene.js, audio.js, main.js, credits, and the build step. The test and wall spam will be fixed quickly first.\n\nThe invert test data needs an Object.is fix. The menuY inversion logic is verified: a 0.8 stick value correctly becomes -1 after applying the Y-axis flip before thresholding. A test for horizontal invert behavior is already present.\n\nThe invert logic is confirmed to flow through `actionsFromPad` into the driving actions. For scene.js, `MeshToonNodeMaterial` will be configured with a hex color and gradient map via `setValues`. The launch test does not check rendered pixels, so a black menu would not be caught.\n\nThe launch test will catch console errors, so failures must be fixed rather than swallowed. The import map must precede the bundled module script because it uses bare imports for three, webgpu, and rapier. WebGPURenderer.setSize also requires the canvas to be in the document.\n\nThe esbuild external configuration will keep three, three/webgpu, three/tsl, and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat as external dependencies so they are not bundled. The credits.js file will export an ABOUT_TEXT string that the build script will inject into the HTML title and meta tags. The initial draft of that text will list the two bugs already caught by testing, with room to add more if further issues appear.\n\nThe box respawn test used a delta time larger than the intentional frame cap, and the invert assertion compared 0 against -0 because the stick value fell below the menu threshold. Both were test harness bugs, not product defects. The final bug count will reflect only genuine game issues that testing actually caught.\n\nThe first test run passed eleven suites, including rocket, drift, offroad, items, battle, and physics. The two failures were test harness bugs, not product defects. A lightweight Playwright script will be added to verify the page title, console output, and canvas size without visual review.\n\nThe browser launch script will serve the HTML and open Chromium via Playwright. It waits for the `data-boot` attribute, records console output, checks the page title, verifies a start button exists, and confirms the canvas width matches the client width. A gate message is only acceptable when WebGPU is unavailable; otherwise the title, start control, and canvas size must all be present with no unexpected errors.\n\nThe build script will inline a `style.css` from the dev folder into the bundled HTML. The next files to write are `style.css`, `audio.js`, `scene.js`, `main.js`, `credits.js`, and `build.mjs`. `main.js` will be kept under 900 lines.\n\nThe character-select UI will display four characters as buttons, with the active slot highlighted. Left and right arrows change the selected driver's character, while up and down arrows move focus between drivers. Confirming a slot starts the race immediately if all slots have defaults.\n\nThe player-count screen will use three large buttons. The course screen will show two big buttons with descriptive blurbs. The settings screen will use two toggle rows, confirmed by pressing a dedicated button.\n\nThe title screen will display seven choices, with the first labeled \"Grand Cup\" and an id of `start-game` to serve as the visible start control. Help will list control lines, About will show a scrollable pre-formatted text block, Results will list drivers in order with a cup message and trial time, and Pause will offer Resume and Quit. The page heading will read \"Kartesian Derby\".\n\nThe document title will match the heading. The gate message will read exactly: \"WebGPU and a local server are required. This derby does not start without them.\" A file:// check and renderer failure handler will be added. The ribbon geometry helper is being drafted for scene.js.\n\nThe ribbon geometry helper is complete. It wraps the last vertex back to the first to close the loop and forms quads that connect around the course.\n\nThe scene will use a shoulder ribbon at half wall height and a road ribbon at 0.08 height, with a grass plane beneath everything. Walls will be built as vertical quads using a custom loop.\n\nPillars will be placed every 12 meters along the course where the ground is high enough. Trees will appear every 18 meters outside the walls on both sides, skipping bridge sections. Rocks will be flattened spheres. The start banner will sit at progress zero above the track. A grandstand of boxes will line the outside wall near the start.\n\nBridge lanterns will use six point lights. The arena view uses a floor plane, box walls, and obstacle meshes instead of ribbons. Karts will spawn at the positions defined in race.karts. The sky sphere is placed at the course center with radius 380, BackSide material, and no shadow casting.\n\nThe sky dome will use `depthWrite = false` and `renderOrder = -1` so it does not obscure other objects. A hemisphere light, ambient light, and directional light will be added to prevent the toon material from crushing its bands.\n\nThe lantern-causeway course will use a dimmer ambient light and a warmer directional light. The kart animation function applies a vertical bob to each kart mesh, rotates it by heading, and spins its wheels based on speed plus a countdown bonus.\n\nThe kart animation now rotates each wheel, tilts the driver based on steering, and bobs the driver vertically. A flame effect scales with active boost states, while drifting triggers spark particles.\n\nSpark particles are positioned behind each kart and fade out as they age. The held-item mesh rotates and shifts between trailing and leading kart states. Box meshes are updated to their world positions and toggled on or off based on the race state.\n\nBoxes will bob vertically and rotate slowly around their axes. The scene imports `boxWorldPosition` from `race.js` without pulling in heavy dependencies like `courses` or `items`. A `syncDynamics` helper is being drafted to manage projectile and hazard meshes.\n\nThe `syncDynamics` helper now grows the mesh group on demand and updates each child's visibility and position from its corresponding item. Each mesh will use its own material so colors can vary by kind. Kart body roll is being added via a slight Z rotation based on steering, with Euler order set to YXZ.\n\nEliminated karts will scale down to 0.72 and sit lower on the track. In battle mode, three balloons will float above each kart, with only the active ones visible.\n\nThe two test failures were harness bugs, not product defects. The simulation correctly clamps frame delta time to 0.05 seconds, so the test suite should use realistic steps rather than forcing a 5-second step. The extra 3-second cap inside `stepRace` is an internal safeguard, not a game bug. I will draft the credits and update the issue count.\n\nThe 3-second internal cap in `stepRace` will be removed or raised to 8 seconds. Simulating the full elapsed time after a background tab is more correct than forcing a short step. The respawn test can then use a single 5.3-second call.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9d08ff2c-9a91-49d6-8a87-a210c2595f34-41","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/src/race.js","old_string":"let left = Math.max(0, Math.min(dt, 3));","new_string":"let left = Math.max(0, Math.min(dt, 8));"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9d08ff2c-9a91-49d6-8a87-a210c2595f34-42","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/test/game.test.mjs","old_string":"const pad = { axes: [0.62, 0.44, 0, 0], buttons: Array(16).fill(0) };","new_string":"const pad = { axes: [0.62, 0.8, 0, 0], buttons: Array(16).fill(0) };"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9d08ff2c-9a91-49d6-8a87-a210c2595f34-43","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh__example__v2__cel__free__efficient__on/work/Kartesian-Derby-Mario-Kart-64-for-N64-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261027-1637/src/race.js","old_string":"if (Math.abs(lateral) > limit && !kart.cheat) {\n      const sign = Math.sign(lateral) || 1;\n      const excess = Math.abs(lateral) - limit;\n      kart.x -= sample.right.x * sign * excess;\n      kart.z -= sample.right.z * sign * excess;\n      kart.lateral = sign * limit;\n      kart.speed *= 0.72;\n      kart.heading -= sign * 0.35;\n      pushEvent(race, { type: \"impact\", x: kart.x, z: kart.z, kind: \"wall\" });\n    }","new_string":"if (Math.abs(lateral) > limit && !kart.cheat) {\n      const sign = Math.sign(lateral) || 1;\n      const excess = Math.abs(lateral) - limit;\n      kart.x -= sample.right.x * sign * excess;\n      kart.z -= sample.right.z * sign * excess;\n      kart.lateral = sign * limit;\n      kart.speed *= 0.72;\n      kart.heading -= sign * 0.35;\n      if (!kart.wallLock) {\n        kart.wallLock = 0.4;\n        pushEvent(race, { type: \"impact\", x: kart.x, z: kart.z, kind: \"wall\" });\n      }\n    }\n    if (kart.wallLock > 0) kart.wallLock = Math.max(0, kart.wallLock - 1 / 60);"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1222,"output_tokens":10457,"cache_read_input_tokens":160384,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"6654e05a-e826-42bb-984e-cce217f66f5e"}
