{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_67","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The race has started. The HUD shows 1st place, Lap 1 of 3, 0 km/h, an empty field, and a countdown of 3.\n\nCanvas output matches across both runs. The device lost error was still recorded despite setting recording to False before browser.close(). The event likely fired during evaluate or the race, or the closure captured the variable at call time.\n\nThe device was destroyed mid-session, not merely on close. The race likely lost the device while running. Two WebGPU devices or earlyoom are unlikely causes. Only one renderer exists and it is reused.\n\nDisposing geometries or materials while the renderer is active might destroy the device. Shadow maps or too many lights and objects could also cause it. The console error is not from a previous page because each run uses a fresh browser. The HUD text appearing after the device loss is expected since the HUD is DOM-based, not WebGPU.\n\nThe canvas may have gone black when the device was lost, but pixel grading is not required. The product could still be visually broken if the device dies immediately.\n\nI will add console markers after boot, about, and race to pinpoint exactly when the device loss occurs. I will also listen for uncapturederror events.\n\nThe device lost error appears as the second log line, immediately after the rapier warning during boot. This suggests the failure occurs during scene initialization rather than during the race itself.\n\nThe device lost error might actually fire during browser.close() itself. Since recording is set to False before that call, the event handler should not capture it unless the lambda default-binds the recording variable differently.\n\nThe device lost error fired during the page session, not during browser.close(). The HUD still updated because it is DOM-based and does not require the GPU. This is a product bug that needs fixing.\n\nI am searching the Three.js source for the exact \"Device was destroyed\" message. Likely culprits include excessive shader compilations, a badly resized canvas, or calling renderer.dispose(). The menu scene is disposed while the race scene is active, and that may crash the device if geometries are freed while the GPU is still using them.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8d449b09-cf65-40ba-b842-810daf14061c-84","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"Device was destroyed|Device Lost","path":"/tmp/grok-goal-17adb0589bdc/implementer/three.webgpu.js","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1231,"output_tokens":1120,"cache_read_input_tokens":125568,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a17fba-2fc3-7d53-9fa6-699993e68862","uuid":"087cbbc8-232c-4275-ab6f-44d91f304e27"}
